1. `Description` should have a maximum length of `DescriptionMaxLength` and a minimum length of `DescriptionMinLength`.
2. `RevenueUSD` should be a non-null `long` value within the range of 1 to 999,999,999,999,999.
3. `CEO` should have a maximum length of `NameMaxLength` and a minimum length of `NameMinLength`.

You need to implement the validation logic for the `Company` class properties based on the given rules.

Write a method `ValidateCompany` that takes a `Company` object as input and returns a list of error messages for any validation failures. If a property violates the validation rules, the error message should indicate the property name and the specific validation rule that was violated.

Use the provided code snippet as a reference for the validation rules and property names.

```csharp
public class Company
{
    public const int DescriptionMaxLength = 100;
    public const int DescriptionMinLength = 10;
    public const int NameMaxLength = 50;
    public const int NameMinLength = 2;

    [Required]
    [StringLength(maximumLength: DescriptionMaxLength, MinimumLength = DescriptionMinLength)]
    public string Description { get; set; }

    [Range(typeof(long), "1", "999999999999999")]
    [Display(Name = "Revenue in USD")]
    public long? RevenueUSD { get; set; }

    [Required]
    [StringLength(NameMaxLength, MinimumLength = NameMinLength)]
    public string CEO { get; set; }
}
```

Implement the `ValidateCompany` method to enforce the specified validation rules and return a list of error messages for any validation failures.

answer:
```csharp
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.ComponentModel.DataAnnotations;

public class CompanyValidator
{
    public List<string> ValidateCompany(Company company)
    {
        var validationResults = new List<ValidationResult>();
        var validationContext = new ValidationContext(company);

        if (!Validator.TryValidateObject(company, validationContext, validationResults, true))
        {
            var errorMessages = new List<string>();
            foreach (var validationResult in validationResults)
            {
                errorMessages.Add(validationResult.ErrorMessage);
            }
            return errorMessages;
        }

        var errorMessagesCustom = new List<string>();

        if (company.Description.Length < Company.DescriptionMinLength || company.Description.Length > Company.DescriptionMaxLength)
        {
            errorMessagesCustom.Add($"Description length should be between {Company.DescriptionMinLength} and {Company.DescriptionMaxLength} characters.");
        }

        if (company.RevenueUSD < 1 || company.RevenueUSD > 999999999999999)
        {
            errorMessagesCustom.Add("Revenue in USD should be within the range of 1 to 999,999,999,999,999.");
        }

        if (company.CEO.Length < Company.NameMinLength || company.CEO.Length > Company.NameMaxLength)
        {
            errorMessagesCustom.Add($"CEO name length should be between {Company.NameMinLength} and {Company.NameMaxLength} characters.");
        }

        return errorMessagesCustom;
    }
}
```

The `CompanyValidator` class contains the `ValidateCompany` method, which first uses the built-in `Validator.TryValidateObject` method to perform standard validation based on data annotations. If any standard validation fails, the corresponding error messages are added to the list. Then, custom validation rules are applied for each property, and error messages are added to the list if any custom validation fails. The method returns a list of error messages for any validation failures.

