answer:
```csharp
using System;
using System.Reflection;

namespace DuckTypingExample
{
    public class DuckTyping
    {
        public T InvokeMethod<T>(object obj, string methodName)
        {
            MethodInfo method = obj.GetType().GetMethod(methodName);
            if (method != null && method.ReturnType == typeof(T))
            {
                return (T)method.Invoke(obj, null);
            }
            else
            {
                return default(T);
            }
        }
    }
}

// Sample usage
public class SampleClass
{
    public void Quack()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Quack!");
    }
}

public class Program
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        DuckTyping duckTyping = new DuckTyping();
        SampleClass duck = new SampleClass();

        duckTyping.InvokeMethod<object>(duck, "Quack"); // Output: Quack!
        duckTyping.InvokeMethod<object>(duck, "Swim"); // Output: (no output as "Swim" method does not exist)
    }
}
```

In the solution, the `InvokeMethod` method uses reflection to retrieve the method from the object and then invokes it if it exists and has the correct return type. If the method does not exist or has a different return type, the method returns the default value for the specified return type. The sample usage demonstrates invoking the `Quack` method on a `SampleClass` object using the `DuckTyping` class.

