In PascalABC.NET, you can use a combination of `Random` class to generate random characters and numbers, `StringBuilder` to store the characters and a `HashSet` to store and check for unique character sequences. Here is a simple implementation:

```CSharp

using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;
using System.Text;

class Program
{
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        HashSet<string> uniqueStrings = new HashSet<string>();
        Random random = new Random();
        const string chars = "abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ1234567890";
        int numberOfSequences = 10; //change this value as per your needs

        for(int i = 0; i < numberOfSequences; i++)
        {
            StringBuilder result = new StringBuilder();
            do
            {
                result.Clear();
                for (int j = 0; j < 8; j++)
                {
                    result.Append(chars[random.Next(chars.Length)]);
                }
            }
            while (uniqueStrings.Contains(result.ToString()));
            uniqueStrings.Add(result.ToString());
        }

        //If you want to print the unique sequences
        foreach (string sequence in uniqueStrings)
            Console.WriteLine(sequence);
    }
}
```

In the above code, a for loop generates `numberOfSequences` sequences. It uses another loop in the scope of the first one for creating characters that are appended to the string. It continues to generate new sequences until a unique sequence is found. This sequence is then added to the HashSet.

With this approach, the time complexity depends on the number of sequences you want to generate. If there is a collision (i.e., a duplicate sequence is generated), the inner loop will have to re-generate a new sequence. But since the sequence generation is random and the total possible unique sequences are large (62^8), the probability of collision happening too often is low. Therefore, it can be considered efficient.

