Mutex stands for Mutual Exclusion Object, which allows multiple program threads to share the same resource, like access to a file, but not simultaneously. When a program thread takes control of a mutex, it is said to have locked the mutex (or owns the lock) and any other thread that needs to access the controlled resource has to wait until the owner of the lock releases it.

For your problem, you can use a Mutex to check if an instance of your application is already running and activate the first instance while stopping the second one from loading.

Here's a sample code in Main method of your application:

```csharp

static class Program
{
    static Mutex mutex = new Mutex(true, "{8F6F0AC4-B9A1-45fd-A8CF-72F04E6BDE8F}");
    [STAThread]
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        if (mutex.WaitOne(TimeSpan.Zero, true))
        {
            var app = new App();
            app.InitializeComponent();
            app.Run();
            mutex.ReleaseMutex();
        }
        else
        {
            // send signal to existing app
            NativeMethods.PostMessage
                ((IntPtr)NativeMethods.HWND_BROADCAST, NativeMethods.WM_SHOWME, IntPtr.Zero, IntPtr.Zero);
        }
    }
}
```

You can notice that we create a named Mutex which tries to gain exclusive access. If it gets it (i.e., no other instance is running) then it starts the WPF application. If not (i.e., another instance is running), it posts a message to the running instance.

`NativeMethods` is a class defined as:

```csharp
public static class NativeMethods
{
    public const int HWND_BROADCAST = 0xffff;
    public static readonly int WM_SHOWME = RegisterWindowMessage("WM_SHOWME");
    [DllImport("user32")]
    public static extern bool PostMessage(IntPtr hwnd, int msg, IntPtr wparam, IntPtr lparam);
    [DllImport("user32")]
    public static extern int RegisterWindowMessage(string message);
}
```

Here we are registering a custom window message "WM_SHOWME". Now it is up to our main instance to listen for this message. That can be done by overriding `WndProc` in the main window:

```csharp
protected override void WndProc(ref Message m)
{
    if(m.Msg == NativeMethods.WM_SHOWME)
    {
        if (IsMinimized) WindowState = WindowState.Normal;
        var top = Topmost;
        Topmost = true;
        Topmost = top;
    }
    base.WndProc(ref m);
}
```

When the message is received, we are restoring application window and making it the topmost window. This should serve your purpose. 

