Here is a solution for your problem in PascalABC.NET using both recursion and the Sieve of Eratosthenes method. First, I will define a function to check if a number is prime or not. Then, I will use this function in a recursive function to multiply all prime numbers not exceeding the specified N:

```PascalABC.NET
using System;

public class Program
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(PrimeProduct(50));
    }

    // Function to check if a number is prime
    public static bool IsPrime(int number)
    {
        if (number <= 1) return false;
        if (number == 2) return true;
        if (number % 2 == 0) return false;

        var boundary = (int)Math.Floor(Math.Sqrt(number));

        for (int i = 3; i <= boundary; i += 2)
            if (number % i == 0)
                return false;

        return true;
    }

    // Recursive function to calculate the product of all primes not greater than n
    public static long PrimeProduct(int n)
    {
        // Base case
        if (n < 2)
            return 1;

        if (IsPrime(n))
        {
            // Multiply n with the prime product of previous number
            return n * PrimeProduct(n - 1);
        }
        else
        {
            // If n is not prime, find the prime product of previous number
            return PrimeProduct(n - 1);
        }
    }
}
```

Please note that this solution might work fine for smaller numbers, but eventually will reach a point where the long data type will not be able to store the result for very large prime products.

