public double CalculatePolygonArea(List<Point> vertices)
{
    int count = vertices.Count;

    if (count < 3)
    {
        return 0; // Not a polygon
    }

    double area = 0.0;
    for (int i = 0; i < count - 1; i++)
    {
        area += (vertices[i].X * vertices[i + 1].Y) - (vertices[i + 1].X * vertices[i].Y);
    }

    // For the edge from the last vertex to the first one
    area += (vertices[count - 1].X * vertices[0].Y) - (vertices[0].X * vertices[count - 1].Y);

    return Math.Abs(area / 2.0);
}
```

This method uses the Shoelace formula to calculate the area of a polygon given its vertices. The vertices should be provided in order, either clockwise or counter-clockwise.

The Shoelace formula works by calculating the sum of the products of the x-coordinates and the next y-coordinates, minus the sum of the products of the y-coordinates and the next x-coordinates. The absolute value of half of this total gives the area of the polygon.

It is also noted that if count of vertices is less than 3, we should return 0 as it is not a polygon.

