Is there a way to test if an object is a dictionary?

In a method I'm trying to get a value from a selected item in a list box.  In some circumstances, the list box might be bound to a dictionary, but this isn't known at compile time.

I would like to do something similar to this:


if (listBox.ItemsSource is Dictionary<??>)
{
    KeyValuePair<??> pair = (KeyValuePair<??>)listBox.SelectedItem;
    object value = pair.Value;
}



Is there a way to do this dynamically at runtime using reflection?  I know it's possible to use reflection with generic types and determine the key/value parameters, but I'm not sure if there's a way to do the rest after those values are retrieved.

answer:
Yes, you can use reflection to do this. In particular, you can use the non-generic `IDictionary` interface:

```csharp
if (listBox.ItemsSource is IDictionary)
{
    var pair = (DictionaryEntry) listBox.SelectedItem;
    var value = pair.Value;
}
```

Normally, generic dictionaries (like `Dictionary<K,V>`) also implement the non-generic `IDictionary` interface, so this should work fine. This method doesn't require you to know in advance what types `K` and `V` are, because the `DictionaryEntry` value returned by `SelectedItem` is itself not generic.

Note, however, that this will only work for dictionaries. If your `ItemsSource` could potentially be some other kind of collection of `KeyValuePair`s that is not a dictionary, you would need a more complex solution.

When a callback is performed, only the control that initializes the callback is loaded into memory and has its state automatically restored. Other controls on the page are not automatically part of the callback mechanism. As a result, their state will not be restored and they will be in their initial state.

If you need the properties of your custom server control to be persisted in a callback, then one solution could be to store the necessary data in a hidden field in your page or within a Session or ViewState. Then you can retrieve this data in the callback and set the properties of your control. 

Here is an example of how to use ViewState:

```csharp
public string MyProperty
{
   get
   {
      return (string)ViewState["MyProperty"];
   }
   set
   {
      ViewState["MyProperty"] = value;
   }
}
```

In this way, ASP.NET will automatically handle persisting the value of your property across postbacks/callbacks. Replace "MyProperty" with whatever your property name is.

This is a fundamental aspect of the ASP.NET page life cycle and callback/postback mechanism. For full control over restoring and persisting state, you might need to override the LoadViewState() and SaveViewState() methods in your controls. 

Remember that storing too much data in ViewState (or Session) can quickly lead to performance problems. So try to store just the minimum information you need to correctly restore the state of your controls.

Another option could be converting your custom server control to an User Control (.ascx). User Control has more control over ViewState across postbacks/callbacks. Also, if your control is highly dependent on ViewState, it makes sense to convert the control to User Control.

