Here is a simple PascalABC.NET program that can calculate the Fibonacci number at a specific position in the sequence. 

This function uses Recursion to solve the problem. Here is the source code of the function:

```csharp
using System;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        Console.Write("Enter the position of the Fibonacci sequence: ");
        int position = Convert.ToInt32(Console.ReadLine());

        int result = Fibonacci(position);

        Console.WriteLine("The Fibonacci number at position {0} is {1}", position, result);

        Console.ReadKey();
    }

    static int Fibonacci(int n)
    {
        if (n <= 1)
            return n;
        else
            return Fibonacci(n - 1) + Fibonacci(n - 2);
    }
}
```

Please note that this function works perfectly for smaller inputs but as the input number grows, the function will take a lot of time to execute because of the enormous amount of unnecessary calculations.

Alternatively, you can use the iterative method (looping) to find Fibonacci at the given index in a much efficient way. Here is how you can do it:

```csharp
using System;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        Console.Write("Enter the position of the Fibonacci sequence: ");
        int position = Convert.ToInt32(Console.ReadLine());

        int result = Fibonacci(position);

        Console.WriteLine("The Fibonacci number at position {0} is {1}", position, result);

        Console.ReadKey();
    }

    static int Fibonacci(int n)
    {
        int a = 0, b = 1, c = 0;

        if (n == 0)
            return a;

        for(int i = 2; i <= n; i++)
        {
            c = a + b;
            a = b;
            b = c;
        }

        return b;
    }
}
```

In this program, we first get the position for which we want to calculate Fibonacci number from the user. Then we calculate the number using Fibonacci method and display the number.

Currently, PascalABC.NET does not directly provide a way to add custom behaviour to the auto property get/set methods.

