In PascalABC.NET 7.0 and later, you can simplify your code using `CallerMemberName` attribute and `nameof` expression to eliminate hard coding the property name every time when calling `PropertyChanged` 

Here is a typical INotifyPropertyChanged implementation

```csharp
public class NotifyPropertyChanged : INotifyPropertyChanged
{
    private int _myProperty;

    public int MyProperty
    {
        get { return _myProperty; }
        set
        {
            _myProperty = value;
            OnPropertyChanged();
        }
    }

    public event PropertyChangedEventHandler PropertyChanged;

    protected virtual void OnPropertyChanged([CallerMemberName] string propertyName = null)
    {
        PropertyChanged?.Invoke(this, new PropertyChangedEventArgs(propertyName));
    }
}
```

With the latest PascalABC.NET compilers and .NET cores, there are few nuGet packages and source generators which can help in simplifying the code, Fody.PropertyChanged and NotifyPropertyChanged.SourceGenerator being most popular among them.

Another approach could be using Aspect Oriented Programming (AOP) Frameworks such as PostSharp which allow you to add notification to properties by just attching an attribute [NotifyPropertyChanged] to the class. However these solutions require additional dependencies which may be overkill for simple cases.

As of now, language construct for custom behavior in property getters and setters is not yet a feature of PascalABC.NET. We have to use these workarounds till those features possibly become part of the language in future.

