answer:
The PascalABC.NET equivalent of the provided Go code is shown below. Implementing this in PascalABC.NET requires some modifications because while Go supports slices and native sorting of slices, PascalABC.NET does not.

In PascalABC.NET, we create a `List<int>` to serve the purpose of Go's slices, and use LINQ to sort the list. Also, since the `foreach` loop in PascalABC.NET does not have an index like Go's `range`, we add a conditional in the nested loop to avoid multiplying a number by itself.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

class Program
{
    static List<int> AbsoluteProductExceptSelf(List<int> nums)
    {
        if (nums.Count == 0)   
            return new List<int>();

        if (nums.Count == 1)
            return new List<int>{1};

        List<int> output = Enumerable.Repeat(1, nums.Count).ToList();

        for(int i=0;i<nums.Count;i++)
        {
            for(int j=0;j<nums.Count;j++)
            {
                if(i != j)
                {
                    output[i] *= nums[j];
                }
            }
            output[i] = Math.Abs(output[i]);
        }

        return output.OrderByDescending(x => x).ToList();
    }

    static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(String.Join(", ", AbsoluteProductExceptSelf(new List<int>{})));   // []
        Console.WriteLine(String.Join(", ", AbsoluteProductExceptSelf(new List<int>{1})));   // [1]
        Console.WriteLine(String.Join(", ", AbsoluteProductExceptSelf(new List<int>{1, 2, 3, 4})));  // [24, 12, 8, 6]
        Console.WriteLine(String.Join(", ", AbsoluteProductExceptSelf(new List<int>{-1, -2, 3, -4}))); // [24, 24, 12, 12]
    }
}
```

