To handle a custom exception with specific error codes and messages in a multithreaded PascalABC.NET program, follow these steps:

1. Define the custom exception class with your specific error codes and messages.
2. Implement a try-catch block in the methods that can throw the custom exception.
3. Implement proper synchronization for accessing shared resources, if needed, to avoid race conditions.
4. If you have multiple threads, use `Task.Run()` or other appropriate techniques to create your threads, and use `task.Wait()` or `await` for waiting for the threads to finish.

Here is an example:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

class CustomException : Exception
{
    public int ErrorCode { get; set; }

    public CustomException(int errorCode, string message) : base(message)
    {
        ErrorCode = errorCode;
    }
}

class Program
{
    static Random _random = new Random();
    static object _syncLock = new object();

    static void Main()
    {
        List<Task> tasks = new List<Task>();

        for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++)
        {
            var task = Task.Run(() => PerformTask(i));
            tasks.Add(task);
        }

        Task.WaitAll(tasks.ToArray());
    }

    static void PerformTask(int taskId)
    {
        try
        {
            DoWork(taskId);
        }
        catch (CustomException ex)
        {
            lock (_syncLock)
            {
                Console.WriteLine($"Task {taskId}: Error code - {ex.ErrorCode}, Message - {ex.Message}");
            }
        }
    }

    static void DoWork(int taskId)
    {
        // Simulate an operation that could fail
        int randomValue = _random.Next(1, 100);

        if (randomValue % 2 == 0)
        {
            throw new CustomException(randomValue, $"Error from task #{taskId}: Operation failed.");
        }
        else
        {
            lock (_syncLock)
            {
                Console.WriteLine($"Task #{taskId}: Operation succeeded.");
            }
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, we have a `CustomException` class with specific error codes and messages. The method `DoWork` can throw a custom exception, so we wrap it with a try-catch block in the `PerformTask` method. We use `Task.Run()` to create 10 threads and execute each task, while waiting for all of them to finish using `Task.WaitAll()`. To avoid race conditions when writing to the console, we use a `lock` to synchronize the access to the Console object.

