First, you need to create a new class globally in Web API to handle this task. This class will implement `System.Web.Http.Filters.IActionFilter`, which gives you access to HTTP action context right before and after the action is executed.

```csharp
public class CheckHeaderFilter : IActionFilter
{
    public bool AllowMultiple => false;

    public Task<HttpResponseMessage> ExecuteActionFilterAsync(HttpActionContext actionContext, CancellationToken cancellationToken, Func<Task<HttpResponseMessage>> continuation)
    {
        const string requiredHeader = "x-required-header";

        if (!actionContext.Request.Headers.Contains(requiredHeader))
        {
            var response = new HttpResponseMessage(HttpStatusCode.PreconditionFailed)
            {
                Content = new StringContent("Missing required header.")
            };

            var tsc = new TaskCompletionSource<HttpResponseMessage>();
            tsc.SetResult(response);
            return tsc.Task;
        }

        return continuation();
    }
}
```
Next, register this filter globally, such as in the `WebApiConfig` class typically found in the App_Start folder:

```csharp
public static class WebApiConfig
{
    public static void Register(HttpConfiguration config)
    {
        // Other configuration code...

        config.Filters.Add(new CheckHeaderFilter());
    }
}
```
Now, every time a request comes in, it will first pass through your `CheckHeaderFilter`, which will check for the presence of the specified header. If the header is absent, it will immediately return a 412 Precondition Failed response; if the header is present, the request will proceed to the controller action.
