    public static string[] SmartStringManipulation(string s, string ignoredWord, int upperBound)
    {
        var words = Regex.Matches(s, @"\b\w+\b").Cast<Match>().Select(m => m.Value).Where(word => !word.Equals(ignoredWord)).ToList();
        var countWord = string.Join("", words);
        Dictionary<char, int> counts = countWord.GroupBy(c => c).ToDictionary(grp => grp.Key, grp => grp.Count());

        var finalList = words.Where(word => word.All(char => counts[char] <= upperBound)).Distinct().OrderBy(word => word).ToArray();
        return finalList;
    }
}
```

Please note, the `\b\w+\b` regex pattern in PascalABC.NET matches `\b\w+\b` in python, and RegEx.Matches method returns collection of Matches, which later be cast as `IEnumerable<Match>` and selected the `Value` from each `Match` and then filtered out the `ignoredWord`.

Also, in Dictionary instantiation, `GroupBy` is used to group by each character and `ToDictionary` is used to convert the return result to `Dictionary<char, int>`.

Additionally, in the `finalList` LINQ query, `Distinct` is used to remove duplicates and `OrderBy` will sort the list based on string comparison. Because PascalABC.NET is statically typed language, the final list is cast to array `ToArray`.

You need a way for your service call to talk back to the UI thread. This is normally done via the use of a Dispatcher. However, since you can't access the Dispatcher directly from your DLL, you can return to the UI thread by using SynchronizationContext.

Here's a brief example of how to use the SynchronizationContext in your scenario:

```csharp
// Save the current synchronization context (captured on the UI thread)
SynchronizationContext uiContext = SynchronizationContext.Current;

// Your service call
Task.Run(() =>
{
      // Do service work here...

      // Call back to the UI thread to update the data object
      uiContext.Post(e =>
      {
        // Update the image URL here
      }, null);
});
```
In the above example, the `SynchronizationContext.Current` returns the synchronization context for the current thread, which, executed in the UI thread, would retain that context. Then, when you update your data object inside `uiContext.Post()`, it's marshaled back to the UI thread, avoiding cross-thread exceptions.  

Remember to capture the SynchronizationContext (with SynchronizationContext.Current) in the UI thread before going to the background thread. The SynchronizationContext.Current would be null in a background thread.

There are a few different ways to do this, but the best solution would involve observing the `IsChecked` properties of each individual `CheckBox` and updating the "All" `CheckBox` accordingly. This could be achieved with a ViewModel that handles the logic behind the `CheckBox` states.

Here's a simple ViewModel to demonstrate:

```csharp
public class ViewModel : INotifyPropertyChanged
{
    private bool? _allChecked = false;

    public bool? AllChecked
    {
        get => _allChecked;
        set
        {
            if (_allChecked == value)
                return;

            _allChecked = value;
            AllCheckedChanged(value);
            NotifyPropertyChanged();
        }
    }

    public ObservableCollection<ItemViewModel> Items { get; } = new ObservableCollection<ItemViewModel>
    {
        new ItemViewModel("A"),
        new ItemViewModel("B"),
        new ItemViewModel("C"),
    };

    public ViewModel()
    {
        // subscribe to each item's PropertyChanged
        foreach (var item in Items)
        {
            item.PropertyChanged += Item_PropertyChanged;
        }
    }

    private void Item_PropertyChanged(object sender, PropertyChangedEventArgs e)
    {
        // if any of the items' IsChecked changes, check if they're all the same
        var areAllChecked = Items.All(item => item.IsChecked);
        var areAllUnchecked = Items.All(item => !item.IsChecked);

        // set AllChecked to null (indeterminate) if the items' IsChecked are not all the same
        AllChecked = areAllChecked
            ? true
            : (areAllUnchecked
                ? false
                : null);
    }

    private void AllCheckedChanged(bool? value)
    {
        // set all items' IsChecked to the AllChecked state, if it's not indeterminate
        if (value.HasValue)
        {
            foreach (var item in Items)
            {
                item.IsChecked = value.Value;
            }
        }
    }

    public event PropertyChangedEventHandler PropertyChanged;
    protected void NotifyPropertyChanged([CallerMemberName] string propertyName = null)
    {
        PropertyChanged?.Invoke(this, new PropertyChangedEventArgs(propertyName));
    }
}

public class ItemViewModel : INotifyPropertyChanged
{
    public string Text { get; }

    private bool _isChecked = false;
    public bool IsChecked
    {
        get => _isChecked;
        set
        {
            if (_isChecked == value)
                return;

            _isChecked = value;
            NotifyPropertyChanged();
        }
    }

    public ItemViewModel(string text)
    {
