Here is the algorithm in PascalABC.NET that can be used to determine if a 2D array is sorted in ascending order both row-wise and column-wise:

```PascalABC.NET
static bool IsSorted(int[,] arr)
{
    int rowCount = arr.GetLength(0);
    int colCount = arr.GetLength(1);

    //Check row-wise
    for (int row = 0; row < rowCount; row++)
    {
        for (int col = 0; col < colCount-1; col++)
        {
            if (arr[row, col] > arr[row, col + 1])
            {
                return false;
            }
        }
    }

    //Check column-wise
    for (int col = 0; col < colCount; col++)
    {
        for (int row = 0; row < rowCount - 1; row++)
        {
            if (arr[row, col] > arr[row + 1, col])
            {
                return false;
            }
        }
    }

    return true;   
}

static void Main()
{
    int[,] arr = new int[,] { {1, 2, 3}, 
                              {4, 5, 6}, 
                              {7, 8, 9}};

    Console.WriteLine(IsSorted(arr) ? "Sorted" : "Not sorted");
}
```
In this algorithm, first we find out the row and column counts using the `GetLength()` method. Then, we check each row to see if the current element is larger than the next element. If it is, we return false, indicating that the array is not sorted in ascending order. Otherwise, we do the same with columns. If no pair of elements breaks the ascending order, we return true, indicating that the array is sorted.

The Newtonsoft.Json library does not auto-detect node types from XML. 

The string of XML to Json will always be interpreted by Newtonsoft.Json as a string type. If you want it to be a decimal then you must do post-processing to remove the quotes.

You could alter your XML document to provide type information in a format that can be interpreted by Newtonsoft.Json library, but this is not supported generally. 

Here's a way to remove the quotes:

```csharp
XmlDocument doc = new XmlDocument();
doc.LoadXml(xmlString);

string jsonString = JsonConvert.SerializeXmlNode(doc, Newtonsoft.Json.Formatting.Indented, true);
JObject json = JObject.Parse(jsonString);

if (json["decimal"] != null)
{
    string decimalString = json["decimal"].ToString();
    if (decimal.TryParse(decimalString, out decimal decimalValue))
    {
        json["decimal"] = decimalValue;
    }
}

string finalJsonString = json.ToString(Newtonsoft.Json.Formatting.Indented);
```

In this string, we parse the JSON string back into a JObject, check if the "decimal" property exists, if it does, try parsing it as a decimal, if that succeeds, replace the string property with a decimal property. 

This assumes that your entire document only contains one "decimal" property. If this is not the case, or if you have multiple properties that could be decimal values, you will have to adjust the logic to handle these cases.

