I have situation like this. Is it correct? If not how to do this correct?

namespace Chess
{
    public abstract class Figure
    {
        public bool SomeMethod()
        {
            return true;
        }
    }

    public class Pawn : Figure
    {
        public new bool SomeMethod()
        {
            return false;
        }
    }

    public class Board
    {

        public void SomeBoardMethod()
        {
            Figure figure = new Pawn();
            var result1 = figure.SomeMethod();
            var result2 = figure.GetType().GetMethod(nameof(Figure.SomeMethod)).Invoke(this, new object[] { });
        }
    }
}


result1 will be true
result2 will be false
Is `figure.GetType().GetMethod(nameof(Figure.SomeMethod)).Invoke(this, new object[] { });` correct?

answer:
No, it is not correct.

Let me explain, `this` in:
```Invoke(this, new object[] { });``` 
refers to an instance of the Board class, not the Figure or Pawn class. That's why it's incorrect and won't work as you might expect. 

Instead, you should use the `figure` object:
```Invoke(figure, new object[] { });```
like this:

```csharp
var result2 = figure.GetType().GetMethod(nameof(Figure.SomeMethod)).Invoke(figure, null);
```
This will return `true` because by using reflection you're actually calling the base class (Figure) method. The `new` keyword in `Pawn`'s `SomeMethod()` is hiding the base `Figure`'s `SomeMethod()` instead of overriding it, therefore when you use reflection to get `SomeMethod()`, it will always refer to the base method.

If you want `Pawn`'s `SomeMethod()` to be called, you have to use `override` keyword in the derived class `Pawn` and `virtual` keyword in the base class `Figure` as below:

```csharp
public abstract class Figure
{
    public virtual bool SomeMethod()
    {
        return true;
    }
}

public class Pawn : Figure
{
    public override bool SomeMethod()
    {
        return false;
    }
}
```
Now, when you use reflection to get `SomeMethod()` and invoke it as below:

```csharp
var result2 = figure.GetType().GetMethod(nameof(Figure.SomeMethod)).Invoke(figure, null);
```
It will return `false` because it's calling the overridden method in `Pawn`.

