answer:
To map all requests (GET, POST, PUT, etc.) and all paths to execute the same method in ASP.NET Core, you can use application middleware instead of `Map` method. Here's how you might do it:

```csharp
var application = WebApplication.Create();

application.Use(async (context, next) =>
{
    await context.Response.WriteAsync("This is all I do!");
    await next();
});

application.Run("http://localhost:8080");
```

The `Use` method is more suitable than `Map` for this task because it allows you to catch all incoming HTTP requests regardless of the HTTP method (GET/POST/PUT...) or the route path.

Note that `next` is not needed in this case since you are not forwarding request to another middleware. So you can remove it.

```csharp
var application = WebApplication.Create();

application.Use(async context =>
{
    await context.Response.WriteAsync("This is all I do!");
});

application.Run("http://localhost:8080");
```

Remember to place your middleware registration before any other routing configuration in your `Configure` method. ASP.NET Core processes middleware in the order they're added in the `Configure` method of the `Startup` class.

