There is a built-in feature in .NET to encrypt sections of your web.config file. You don't need any extra plan, command line switch, or to store values in the registry. You can do this programmatically, right from your code. What you're looking for is called Protected Configuration.

Here is a simple example of how to use it:

```csharp
// Open the Web.config file.
System.Configuration.Configuration config = 
  WebConfigurationManager.OpenWebConfiguration("~");

// Get the section to protect.
ConfigurationSection section = 
  config.GetSection("connectionStrings");

if (section != null)
{
  // Protect (encrypt)the section.
  section.SectionInformation.ProtectSection(
    "DataProtectionConfigurationProvider");

  // Save the encrypted section.
  config.Save();
}
```

In the above code, "connectionStrings" is the name of the section you want to encrypt. You can replace it with the name of your desired section. "DataProtectionConfigurationProvider" is one of the two built-in providers for encrypting configuration sections.

Remember, this is symmetric encryption; the .NET framework automatically handles the decryption for you when you read the values. 

To use this framework, you should have full trust mode in your ASP.NET application. If your hosting environment is in medium or partial trust mode, then you probably can't use this feature. You should check your ASP.NET trust level with your hosting provider before using this feature. 

And the last but not least, you don't need to worry about where the encryption key is stored or if you need to backup it when migrating to another server because .NET handles this behind the scenes for you. The encryption key is kept in the local machine store which is secured by ACLs (Access Control Lists).

