There is no straight forward API call that can serve this purpose. However, you can use the following workaround.

The time at which the current user logged in can be found in the Security event log. So the script would need to look there, find the last login event for the current user and then compare the time of that event to the current time. 

This would be quite complex and slow if done purely in a batch file (as it would involve parsing the entire event log line by line), but could be done fairly easily and quickly in a programming language like PascalABC.NET or VB.Net, using the System.Diagnostics.Eventing.Reader.EventLogReader class.

Below is a code path in PascalABC.NET.

```csharp
public DateTime GetLastLoginTime(string userName)
{
    string queryString = string.Format("*[System[EventID=4624] and EventData[Data[@Name='TargetUserName'] and (Data='{0}')]]", userName);
    string logName = "Security";
    DateTime dtNow = DateTime.Now;
    EventRecord entry = null;
    DateTime latestTime = DateTime.MinValue;

    EventLogQuery eventsQuery = new EventLogQuery(logName, PathType.LogName, queryString);

    try
    {
        EventLogReader logReader = new EventLogReader(eventsQuery);

        for (entry = logReader.ReadEvent(); null != entry; entry = logReader.ReadEvent())
        {
            if (entry.TimeCreated.HasValue && entry.TimeCreated.Value > latestTime)
            {
                latestTime = entry.TimeCreated.Value;
            }
        }
    }
    catch (EventLogNotFoundException e)
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Log: {0} not found", logName);
    }

    return latestTime;
}
```

Make sure enable audit logon events in order to log event id 4624 for successful logon events. The important thing is this requires administrative rights to access the security logs.


