In PascalABC.NET, you can use `Type.GetEvent()` method which retrieves the `EventInfo` of the specified event on target object, then use `EventInfo.AddEventHandler()` method to hook up the event with a delegate.

Since `AddEventHandler()` method requires a delegate that matches with the event handler, using `Delegate.CreateDelegate()` method allows you to create a delegate dynamically given the necessary parameters (i.e., method info of the handler method, target object). Below is the example code.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Reflection;

public class Program
{
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        var foo = new Foo();
        DynamicSubscribeEvent(foo, "Bar");

        foo.RaiseEvent();
    }

    public static void DynamicSubscribeEvent(object target, string eventName)
    {
        var eventInfo = target.GetType().GetEvent(eventName);
        if (eventInfo != null)
        {
            var handler = typeof(Program) // Replace this with appropriate class
                .GetMethod("EventHandler", BindingFlags.NonPublic | BindingFlags.Static);
            Delegate del = Delegate.CreateDelegate(eventInfo.EventHandlerType, handler);
            eventInfo.AddEventHandler(target, del);
        }
        else
        {
            throw new ArgumentException($"The {eventName} isn't exist on {target.GetType()}");
        }
    }

    private static void EventHandler(object sender, EventArgs e) // This is the event Handler we need to link.
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Event fired!");
    }
}

public class Foo
{
    public event EventHandler Bar;

    public void RaiseEvent()
    {
        Bar?.Invoke(this, EventArgs.Empty);
    }
}
```

Be careful that the `EventHandler` method has to match the signature of the target event which you want to subscribe to. If you don't know the event signature, you have to write a universal handler method to fit all possible event signature, but please note this may difficult and risk since you may not know what the event will actually send. Refection.Emit or dynamic method building might be a better choice in this case.

Here is a PascalABC.NET program using a dictionary to count character occurrences and a StringBuilder to construct the final string.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Text;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        string str = "azxxzyxa";
        Console.WriteLine(RemoveDuplicates(str));
    }

    static string RemoveDuplicates(string str)
    {
        Dictionary<char, int> charCount = new Dictionary<char, int>();
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();

        for (int i = 0; i < str.Length; i++)
        {
            char c = str[i];
            if (charCount.ContainsKey(c))
            {
                charCount[c]++;
            }
            else
            {
                charCount[c] = 1;
            }
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < str.Length; i++)
        {
            char c = str[i];
            if (charCount[c] == 1)
            {
                sb.Append(c);
            }
        }

        return sb.ToString();
    }
}
```

In this code, the RemoveDuplicates function goes through the input string and counts the occurrences of each character in a dictionary. Then it goes through the string again, appending only those characters which occurred once to the output string.

This program treats different cases as different characters, maintains the original order of non-duplicated characters, and uses optimal data structures to achieve a time complexity of O(n) and a space complexity of O(n) where n is the length of the string.

