Here is a PascalABC.NET solution for the problem. It includes a method CheckArmstrongNumber to check whether a number is an Armstrong number or not, and a method ArmstrongNumbersInRange to identify all the Armstrong numbers in a range.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

public static class ArmstrongHelper
{
    public static bool CheckArmstrongNumber(int number)
    {
        int n = number;
        int digitCount = n.ToString().Count();
        int sum = 0;

        while (n > 0)
        {
            int digit = n % 10;
            sum += (int)Math.Pow(digit, digitCount);
            n /= 10;
        }

        return number == sum;
    }

    public static List<int> ArmstrongNumbersInRange(int start)
    {
        var result = new List<int>();

        for (int num = start; num <= 10000; num++)
        {
            if (CheckArmstrongNumber(num))
            {
                result.Add(num);
            }
        }

        return result.OrderBy(x => x).ToList();
    }
}
```
The method CheckArmstrongNumber computes the Armstrong number using a while loop that iterates the digits in number from right to left once, so its time complexity is O(d), where d is the number of digits in number.

The method ArmstrongNumbersInRange iterates through every number from start up to 10,000 once, and for each number, it calls CheckArmstrongNumber, so its time complexity is O(n*d), where n is the range of the numbers and d is the maximum number of digits of the numbers.

Bonus points:

For considering the Armstrong number using the count of unique digits, we need to modify the CheckArmstrong method because the definition of Armstrong number is different in this scenario. We now need to count unique digits for computation. The implementation of this feature is a little more challenging due to unique digit computations. We can store the digits in a Set data structure while calculating the sum, which will automatically reject duplicate digits because a Set only stores unique values.

In addition, we can offer a feature to switch between the two definitions. For this, we can use an enum to represent the two different definitions and modify the CheckArmstrongNumber method to take an additional argument representing which definition to use.

```csharp
public enum ArmstrongDefinition
{
    TotalDigits,
    UniqueDigits
}

public static class ArmstrongHelper
{
    public static bool CheckArmstrongNumber(int number, ArmstrongDefinition definition)
    {
        int digitCount;
        int sum = 0;
        var digitsSet = new HashSet<int>();
        int n = number;

        while (n > 0)
        {
            int digit = n % 10;
            sum += (int)Math.Pow(digit, definition == ArmstrongDefinition.TotalDigits ? number.ToString().Count() : digitsSet.Count);
            digitsSet.Add(digit);
            n /= 10;
        }

        return number == sum;
    }

    // Rest of the code remains the same
}
```

The time and space complexity should remain the same as before even after these modifications. The only extra space we used is a HashSet to store digits, which in the worst case (all digits are distinct) can be up to 10.

