Here is a PascalABC.NET function that calculates the area of a triangle using Heron's formula and has exception handling to verify if the inputs form a valid triangle. The condition for a valid triangle is that the sum of any two sides must be greater than the third side.

```PascalABC.NET
using System;

public class Program
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        try
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Enter three sides of a triangle:");
            double a = Convert.ToDouble(Console.ReadLine());
            double b = Convert.ToDouble(Console.ReadLine());
            double c = Convert.ToDouble(Console.ReadLine());

            double area = TriangleArea(a, b, c);

            Console.WriteLine("The area of the triangle is: " + area);
        }
        catch (ArgumentException ex)
        {
            Console.WriteLine(ex.Message);
        }
    }

    public static double TriangleArea(double a, double b, double c)
    {
        if (a + b > c && a + c > b && b + c > a)
        {
            double s = (a + b + c) / 2; 
            return Math.Sqrt(s * (s - a) * (s - b) * (s - c)); 
        }
        else
        {
            throw new ArgumentException("The given side lengths do not form a valid triangle.");
        }
    }
}
```

This program takes three side lengths from the user as input. It then runs the `TriangleArea()` function with these lengths and outputs the returned value. If the function detects an invalid triangle, it throws an exception which gets caught and handled by the catch block in the main method. The error message is then printed to the console.

Custom drawing of scrollbars inside other controls (like a Panel) is not directly supported in Windows Forms. The scrollbars that appear are dependent on the system theming.

If you really want to customize the appearance of scrollbars in your controls, you would likely need to create a custom control that does not use the System.Windows.Forms.ScrollBar, but instead draws its own scrollbars, and handles the logic for scrolling.

For this, you would subclass the control you want to modify (or UserControl for a completely custom control) and override the OnPaint method to draw your custom scrollbar. You'd also need to handle the mouse events to allow for user interaction with your custom scrollbar.

Below is a simplified example of how you might begin a custom Panel control with a vertical scrollbar:

```csharp
using System.Drawing;
using System.Windows.Forms;

public class CustomPanel : Panel
{
    private int ScrollValue = 0;
    private const int ScrollbarWidth = 10;

    public CustomPanel()
    {
        SetStyle(ControlStyles.ResizeRedraw, true); // Ensures the panel is redrawn when resized
        this.AutoScroll = false;
    }

    protected override void OnPaint(PaintEventArgs e)
    {
        // Draw your custom scrollbar here
        e.Graphics.FillRectangle(Brushes.LightGray,
                                 new Rectangle(this.Width - ScrollbarWidth, 0, ScrollbarWidth, this.Height));
        e.Graphics.FillRectangle(Brushes.DarkGray,
                                 new Rectangle(this.Width - ScrollbarWidth, ScrollValue * this.Height / 100, ScrollbarWidth, 50));

        // Draw the rest of your control (if applicable)
        base.OnPaint(e);
    }

    protected override void OnScroll(ScrollEventArgs se)
    {
        ScrollValue = se.NewValue; // Update the scrollbar value
        this.Refresh(); // Redraw the control

        base.OnScroll(se);
    }
}
```

Note that this is a very simple and incomplete example, it would work as a starting point but you would still need to handle mouse events for controlling the scrollbar and also take care of the actual scrolling of content.

