This function calculates the median of a list of integers. The median is a number which is in the middle of a list after it has been sorted. If the number of items is even, then it's half of the sum of the two middle numbers.

```PascalABC.NET
using System;
using System.Linq;

public class Program
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(Median(new int[]{3, 1, 2, 4, 5})); // Output: 3
        Console.WriteLine(Median(new int[]{-10, 4, 6, 1000, 10, 20})); // Output: 15
    }

    public static double Median(int[] array)
    {
        // Calculate the size of the array
        int n = array.Length;

        // Sort the array
        Array.Sort(array);

        // Check if n is even or odd.
        // If n is odd, return the value at index n/2.
        // If n is even, return the average of values at indices n/2 - 1 and n/2.
        return n % 2 == 1 ? array[n / 2] : (array[n / 2 - 1] + array[n / 2]) / 2.0;
    }
}
```

It's important to note that integer division in PascalABC.NET works the same as Python, where the result of the division is always rounded down. So, `n / 2` would give the middle index for an odd-length array and the second of the two middle indices for an even-length array.

In PascalABC.NET, most of the array methods are static and located in the Array class, such as the Sort method used here. That's why you see `Array.Sort(array)` instead of `array.Sort()` as you might expect if you're coming from Python or some other languages.

Finally, remember to add `using System.Linq;` to be able to use LINQ (Language Integrated Query) functions like `Array.Sort()`.

