}
```

This way, you make sure your classes and methods have single responsibility and you also hide the implementation details as much as possible.

This technique is using the Interface Segregation Principle and the Dependency Inversion Principle, which are part of the SOLID principles of object-oriented design and programming.

In a Windows Forms application, you can catch all exceptions by handling the Application.ThreadException event. Here's how you set this up typically in the Program.cs:

```csharp
public static void Main()
{
    Application.EnableVisualStyles();
    Application.SetCompatibleTextRenderingDefault(false);

    // Add the event handler for handling UI thread exceptions to the event.
    Application.ThreadException += new ThreadExceptionEventHandler(ErrorHandlerForm_UIThreadException);

    // Set the unhandled exception mode to force all Windows Forms errors to go through our handler.
    Application.SetUnhandledExceptionMode(UnhandledExceptionMode.CatchException);

    // Add the event handler for handling non-UI thread exceptions to the event. 
    AppDomain.CurrentDomain.UnhandledException += new UnhandledExceptionEventHandler(CurrentDomain_UnhandledException);

    Application.Run(new MainForm());
}

private static void ErrorHandlerForm_UIThreadException(object sender, ThreadExceptionEventArgs t)
{
    // Handle your exceptions here
}

private static void CurrentDomain_UnhandledException(object sender, UnhandledExceptionEventArgs e)
{
    // Handle your exceptions here
}
```

The ThreadException event handler is for unhandled exceptions thrown on main UI thread. The UnhandledException event handler is for exceptions thrown in other threads. Remember, if a secondary thread throws an unhandled exception, it will terminate immediately. The application, however, will continue to execute.

Consider the case of exceptions occurring in event handlers where the event was raised from non-UI thread. Those won't be caught by ThreadException, so you need both handlers to cover all your bases. Always remember, it's a best practice to handle exceptions as close as possible to the place they occur.

For user control hosted in IE, first chance exceptions are swallowed by the parent process hosting control (IE in this case). A better approach would be to add error handling on all event entry points to handle exceptions that could originate from control.

