In PascalABC.NET you will need to use the `Regex` class. Note that PascalABC.NET string lenghts are accessed via the `Length` attribute, not a `len` function. The characters in a string are accessed via an indexer, like an array. Here is how you can do it:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;

public class Program
{
    static string FindClosestVowelSubSequence(string word)
    {
        string pattern = @"[^a-zA-Z]*([bcdfghj-np-tv-zBCDFGHJ-NP-TV-Z][aeiouAEIOU]*[bcdfghj-np-tv-zBCDFGHJ-NP-TV-Z][^a-zA-Z]*)[^a-zA-Z]*$";
        Match match = Regex.Match(word, pattern);

        if (match.Success)
        {
            // Find the beginning and end of the vowel subsequence
            int begin = match.Groups[1].Index;
            int end = match.Groups[1].Length;

            // Get the consonants enclosing the subsequence
            char left_c = word[begin];
            char right_c = word[end - 1];

            // Get the subsequence of vowels
            string vowel_subseq = match.Groups[1].Value.Substring(1, match.Groups[1].Value.Length - 2);

            // Return the subsequence of vowels
            string res = left_c + vowel_subseq + right_c;

            return vowel_subseq;
        }
        else
        {
            // If no match found, return an empty string
            return string.Empty;
        }
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        string word = "Hello";
        Console.WriteLine(FindClosestVowelSubSequence(word));
    }
}
```

This code defines a `FindClosestVowelSubSequence` function that mirrors the functionality of the provided Python code. Like the Python version, it first compiles a regular expression using the provided pattern. In PascalABC.NET, this is done using the `Regex` class. It then checks if there's a match in the given word. If there is, it behaves like the Python version: it figures out where the vowel subsequence begins and ends, determines the consonants enclosing it, and returns it. If no match is found, it returns an empty string.

