I have done a bit of research into this and it seems that the only way to sort a data bound combo box is to sort the data source itself (a DataTable in a DataSet in this case).  

If that is the case then the question becomes what is the best way to sort a DataTable?

The combo box bindings are set in the designer initialize using


myCombo.DataSource = this.typedDataSet;
myCombo.DataMember = "Table1";
myCombo.DisplayMember = "ColumnB";
myCombo.ValueMember = "ColumnA";



I have tried setting

this.typedDataSet.Table1.DefaultView.Sort = "ColumnB DESC";


But that makes no difference, I have tried setting this in the control constructor, before and after a typedDataSet.Merge call.

answer:
The issue probably arises from the fact that `DataTable` sorting does not directly affect the `DataTable` itself. This is due to the fact that internal data of `DataTable` is not sorted, rather, a different `DataView` is created which presents the data in a sorted manner.

So, when you set `this.typedDataSet.Table1.DefaultView.Sort = "ColumnB DESC";`, it basically sorts the `DefaultView` of the `DataTable`, but the `DataTable` itself remains unchanged.

To handle sorted display in the ComboBox, you need to assign the sorted `DefaultView` (which is basically a `DataView`) as the data source of the ComboBox:

```csharp
this.typedDataSet.Table1.DefaultView.Sort = "ColumnB DESC";
myCombo.DataSource = this.typedDataSet.Table1.DefaultView; // Assigning sorted DefaultView to ComboBox
myCombo.DisplayMember = "ColumnB";
myCombo.ValueMember = "ColumnA";
```

By doing this, you are telling the ComboBox to bind to the sorted `DataView` and not the actual `DataTable`.

Now, your ComboBox should properly sort the data for display. One thing that is important to note is that it will not affect the data in your `DataTable` and any changes in the ComboBox will reflect back to your `DataTable` data. 

However, if you want to sort the `DataTable` itself, then you might want to consider using `DataView.ToTable` method which creates a new `DataTable` based on sorting. 

```csharp
DataView dv = new DataView(this.typedDataSet.Table1);
dv.Sort = "ColumnB desc";
DataTable sortedTable = dv.ToTable();
```

Remember that this will create a new `DataTable` and will not have any connection to original `DataTable` (changes in new `DataTable` will not reflect back to original `DataTable` and vice versa).

