No, there's no built-in functionality in .NET for this specific purpose. But, you can do it manually by defining your own method. Here's a simple example using `System.IO.Path` methods:

```csharp
public string TrimPath(string path, int maxLength)
{
    if (path.Length <= maxLength) return path;

    string fileName = Path.GetFileName(path);
    string dirName = Path.GetDirectoryName(path);

    while (path.Length > maxLength)
    {
        int lastSeparator = dirName.LastIndexOf(Path.DirectorySeparatorChar);
        if (lastSeparator == -1) break; // Can't trim it further
        dirName = dirName.Substring(0, lastSeparator);
        path = dirName + @"\..." + Path.DirectorySeparatorChar + fileName;
    }

    return path;
}
```

This method will keep trimming directory names from the end of the path until it's below your maximum length or there's no more directories to trim. When it starts trimming, it also adds the ellipsis ("...") to indicate that some part of the path has been omitted.

Please note that this is a very simplistic example and might not meet all your requirements. Verify and test this code before using it in production. It will still display the full path if it can't be trimmed to the specified length without removing the file name.

