Here I am presenting an algorithm demonstrating LRU Cache in PascalABC.NET which will have O(n) complexity for 'Get' and 'Put' operation where n is the capacity of Cache. 

```PascalABC.NET
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class LRUCache {

   private int capacity;
   private Dictionary<int, LinkedListNode<CacheItem>> cache;
   private LinkedList<CacheItem> lruList;

   public LRUCache(int capacity) {
     this.capacity = capacity;
     this.cache = new  Dictionary<int, LinkedListNode<CacheItem>>(capacity);
     this.lruList = new LinkedList<CacheItem>();
   }

   public int Get(int key) {
     if (!cache.ContainsKey(key)) return -1;

     var node = cache[key];
     int value = node.Value.Value;

     lruList.Remove(node);
     lruList.AddLast(node);

     return value;
   }

   public void Put(int key, int value) {
     if (cache.Count >= capacity) {
       RemoveLeastRecentlyUsed();
     }

     CacheItem cacheItem = new CacheItem {Key = key, Value = value};
     var node = new LinkedListNode<CacheItem>(cacheItem);
     lruList.AddLast(node);
     cache.Add(key, node);
   }

   private void RemoveLeastRecentlyUsed() {
     // first item in linked list is LRU item
     var node = lruList.First;
     cache.Remove(node.Value.Key);
     lruList.RemoveFirst();
   }

   class CacheItem {
     public int Key { get; set; }
     public int Value { get; set; }
   } 
}
```

Please note that since insertion and removal from the hashtable/dictionary and linkedlist take constant time i.e. O(1), the time complexity of 'Get' and 'Put' operation in this LRU cache implementation will be O(1) not O(n^2), where n is the capacity of the cache. To achieve a time complexity of O(n^2), you would typically need a nested loop or a recursion within a loop - neither of which is needed in an LRU cache implementation. 

The request for a time complexity of O(n^2) in your question may be a misunderstanding, because LRU cache operations should ideally have time complexity of O(1) which is achieved in above implementation. So in practical usage, you wouldn’t want an LRU cache algorithm with O(n^2) time complexity.

NHibernate avoids making unnecessary database hits by storing entities in a first level cache or a session level cache. This cache is created when a session is opened and destroyed when the session is closed. Any objects retrieved or saved to the database during this session are held in this cache and any retrieval of this data, during the same session, will hit the cache rather than the database. 

If you need to reflect the changes made directly in the database (bypassing NHibernate), you will either need to refresh the entities or clear the session.

Here's on how you can do it:

1. Use `ISession.Refresh(object)` to reload a single entity from the database.

```csharp
var customer = session.Get<Customer>(customerId);
// database is changed by another process 
session.Refresh(customer);
```

2. Use `ISession.Clear()` to remove all the objects from the session cache.

```csharp
var customer = session.Get<Customer>(customerId);
// database is changed by another process 
session.Clear();
customer = session.Get<Customer>(customerId);
```

You may choose to use Evict method if you want to remove specific object from NHibernate cache -

```csharp
session.Evict(customer);
```

Remember that these are not necessarily best practices, as routinely using these could suggest that your caching strategy or session management strategy needs to be revisited, but they can be useful in certain specific scenarios.

Ideally, you should try to manage your session such that changes aren't made directly to the database while a session is open. If this isn't feasible, NHibernate might not be the best tool for your scenario. It might make more sense to use a micro-ORM or pure ADO.NET so you have more control over when and how data is pulled from the database.

