If you are handling significantly large XML data, loading the entire document into a string and then doing a Regex-based clean-up might not be the most memory-efficient approach. 

In such a scenario, creating a custom Stream or TextReader that filters out the problematic characters on-the-fly can be an alternative and more performant solution. This allows your StreamReader to weed out all invalid characters as it is reading the stream, which could potentially be more memory efficient and faster than loading the entire document into memory and running a Regex replace.

Here is an example of a custom TextReader that replaces invalid XML characters:

```csharp
public class XmlSanitizingStream : StreamReader
{
    public XmlSanitizingStream(Stream stream) : base(stream){}

    public override int Peek()
    {
        int c = base.Peek();

        while(IsInvalidXmlChar(Convert.ToChar(c)))
        {
            base.Read();
            c = base.Peek();
        }

        return c;
    }

    public override int Read()
    {
        int c = base.Read();

        while(IsInvalidXmlChar(Convert.ToChar(c)))
        {
            c = base.Read();
        }

        return c;
    }

    private bool IsInvalidXmlChar(char c)
    {
        return !((c == 0x9 || c == 0xA || c == 0xD) ||
                 (c >= 0x20 && c <= 0xD7FF) ||
                 (c >= 0xE000 && c <= 0xFFFD) ||
                 (c >= 0x10000 && c <= 0x10FFFF));
    }
}
```

Now, instead of directly using your Stream with XmlReader, you would wrap it with this sanitizing StreamReader like this:

```csharp
XmlReaderSettings settings = new XmlReaderSettings
{
    CheckCharacters = false
};

using (StreamReader sanitizingReader = new XmlSanitizingStream(yourOriginalStream))
using (XmlReader reader = XmlReader.Create(sanitizingReader, settings))
{
    while (reader.Read())
    {
        // your code here
    }
}
```

This code throws out invalid chars as they are being read from the stream, instead of loading the whole content into a string.
Keep in mind that it might not suit every situation, especially when you need to keep track of original data positions or when invalid characters cannot simply be skipped.

The above technique is not guaranteed to catch every form of invalid XML, but it should handle invalid characters according to the XML specification.

