Yes, it is possible to deal with concurrent file access, you just need to handle them properly using the System.IO classes.

Here's an implementation proposal:

You can use the FileStream class with FileShare parameter set to allow multiple StreamWriter instances writing to the same file concurrently. The FileShare enum specifies the kind of access other FileStream objects have to the same file.

```csharp
public void Log(string message)
{
    FileStream fs = null;

    try {
        fs = new FileStream("log.txt", FileMode.Append, FileAccess.Write, FileShare.Write);
        using (StreamWriter writer = new StreamWriter(fs)) {
            fs = null;
            writer.WriteLine(message);
        }
    }
    finally {
        if(fs != null)
            fs.Dispose();
    }
}
```
Just remember exceptions can still be thrown when trying to access the file (for example: directory not found, drive not ready, etc.), so you should catch them and handle appropriately.

Please also notice that writing to a file from many threads at the same time can still cause data to be written in unexpected order due to the way the operating system schedules threads. You have to design your logging accordingly.

Lastly, it's worth mentioning that even though you had issues with log4net, it's often a good idea to continue trying to get such a library to work. They've usually solved a bunch of edge cases and will save you time and effort in the long run. Just make sure you configure appenders correctly to enable concurrent logging.

You will require to render an HTML form dynamically from your server-sided code with necessary information such as action attribute pointing to your external URL, all the necessary input fields, and some JavaScript to submit the form on document's load event. This way, when the server returns the response (which includes the form) to the client, the browser will automatically submit the form to the specified location, effectively making a POST request and redirecting the user to the external site.

Here's an example method in PascalABC.NET:

```csharp
public void RedirectToExternalUrl(string url, Dictionary<string, string> data)
{
    var formId = "RedirectForm";

    string htmlForm = $"<html><head></head><body><form id=\"{formId}\" name=\"{formId}\" action=\"{url}\" method=\"POST\">";

    foreach (var keyValuePair in data)
    {
        htmlForm += $"<input type=\"hidden\" name=\"{keyValuePair.Key}\" value=\"{keyValuePair.Value}\">";
    }

    htmlForm += "</form>";

    htmlForm += $"<script>document.getElementById(\'{formId}\').submit();</script>";
    htmlForm += "</body></html>";

    Response.Write(htmlForm);
    Response.End();
}
```

