In Linq-to-Entities, the `Where()` extension method expects an `Expression<Func<..., bool>>`, not a `Func<..., bool>`. This is because Entity Framework doesn't actually run your predicate - instead, it converts the Expression Tree back into SQL and runs that against the database. If the predicate was a normal delegate, this wouldn't be possible.

This is why `Expression<Func<...>>` is used - Entity Framework can look at the expression tree and build SQL that will do the equivalent operation. Instead of creating a delegate and invoking the delegate (which is what `Func<...>` would give you), it looks at the code that makes up the method and translates that into SQL.

By using `Expression<Func<User, bool>>? predicate;` instead of `Func<User, bool>? predicate;` , you're still maintaining the ability to have the predicate be null meaning the method would return all Users when the predicate is null.


