Setting the Service Behavior to STA mode can be tricky because WCF services are intrinsically MTA (Multi-threaded Apartment), and there isn't a direct way of setting them to STA.

However, you can achieve this workaround by ensuring the WCF service operations that interact with your UI components run on STA threads. 

To do this, you can use a helper class that will dispatch your operations onto an STA thread. Here’s an example how you could do this:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Threading;
using System.Security.Permissions;

public class StaHelper
{
    private readonly ManualResetEvent _completeEvent = new ManualResetEvent(false);
    private Exception _delegateException;
    private Delegate _delegate;
    private object[] _delegateArgs;

    private StaHelper(Delegate d, params object[] args)
    {
        _delegate = d;
        _delegateArgs = args;
    }

    [SecurityPermission(SecurityAction.LinkDemand)]
    public static void Run(Delegate d, params object[] args)
    {
        StaHelper staOp = new StaHelper(d, args);
        Thread thread = new Thread(staOp.StartThread);
        thread.SetApartmentState(ApartmentState.STA);
        thread.Start();
        staOp._completeEvent.WaitOne();

        if (staOp._delegateException != null)
            throw staOp._delegateException;
    }

    private void StartThread()
    {
        try
        {
            _delegate.DynamicInvoke(_delegateArgs);
        }
        catch (Exception e)
        {
            _delegateException = e;
        }
        finally
        {
            _completeEvent.Set();
        }
    }
}
```

You can use it in your WCF service code as follows:

```csharp
public void YourWcfServiceOperation()
{
    StaHelper.Run(new Action(() => 
    { 
        // WPF UI interaction code here 
    }));
}
```

However, make sure your WCF service operation and the action performed on the UI components are thread safe, as this could create unexpected behaviors.

