This problem can be solved by essentially breaking it down into two smaller tasks:

1. Identifying if a number is prime.
2. Multiplying all the prime numbers less than or equal to n.

We will write a helper function "IsPrime" to check if a number is prime. The function checks if a number has any divisor other than 1 and itself. If it has, then the number is not prime. If it doesn't have, then the number is prime.

After that, we will write a recursive function "MultiplyPrime" that calculates the multiplication of all prime numbers that are less than or equal to a given integer n.

Here is the PascalABC.NET code that does this:

```csharp
using System;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(MultiplyPrime(50));
    }

    // Function to check if a number is prime
    static bool IsPrime(int num, int i = 2)
    {
        if (num <= 2)
            return (num == 2) ? true : false;
        if (num % i == 0)
            return false;
        if (i * i > num)
            return true;

        // Check for next divisor
        return IsPrime(num, i + 1);
    }

    // Recursive function to multiply all prime numbers less than or equal to n
    static long MultiplyPrime(int n)
    {
        if (n <= 1)
            return 1;
        else if (IsPrime(n))
            return n * MultiplyPrime(n - 1);
        else
            return MultiplyPrime(n - 1);
    }
}
```
Remember that the call stack limit in case of recursion is limited and depends on the environment in which your program is running. For large numbers this can lead to a stack overflow error. For such cases an iterative solution might be more appropriate.

Also in this case, keep in mind that the multiplication of a large number of primes might lead to overflow of int or long data types. To overcome this, you might want to use more complex data types that can handle very large numbers, such as the `BigInteger` structure.

Here is a simple implementation in PascalABC.NET for calculating a weighted mean of a set of numbers:

```csharp
public static double CalculateWeightedMean(int[] numbers, int[] weights)
{
    if (numbers.Length != weights.Length)
    {
        throw new ArgumentException("Numbers and weights must have the same length");
    }

    double sumOfNumbers = 0;
    int sumOfWeights = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i < numbers.Length; i++)
    {
        sumOfNumbers += numbers[i] * weights[i];
        sumOfWeights += weights[i];
    }

    return sumOfNumbers / sumOfWeights;
}
```

This function starts by checking whether the arrays of numbers and weights have same length. If not, it throws an `ArgumentException`. 

After that, it calculates the sum of the multiplication of each pair of number and weight (i.e., `sumOfNumbers`). Along with that, it also calculates the sum of all weights (i.e., `sumOfWeights`). 

Finally, it returns the quotient of `sumOfNumbers` by `sumOfWeights`, which is the weighted mean.

To use the function, you could do something like:

```csharp
int[] numbers = { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 };
int[] weights = { 3, 5, 7, 9, 11 };
double weightedMean = CalculateWeightedMean(numbers, weights);
Console.WriteLine("The weighted mean is: " + weightedMean);
```

Remember, the weights should reflect the significance of each number. Higher weights give greater importance to the corresponding number in the final mean.

