You can definitely deploy your Azure Function Apps using the REST API but it is bit complex and multi-step process. 

Here are the steps you need to follow:

Step 1: Authorize the Azure Management REST API
To call any Azure Management REST API, you need to first get an access token from Azure Active Directory (AAD). Unfortunately, this process can't be done by simple HTTP requests, you'll need to make use of Azure SDK.

You can use following PascalABC.NET code to get access token:

```PascalABC.NET
using Microsoft.IdentityModel.Clients.ActiveDirectory;
...
public async Task<string> GetToken(string tenantId, string clientId, string clientSecret)
{
    var context = new AuthenticationContext("https://login.windows.net/" + tenantId);
    ClientCredential clientCredential = new ClientCredential(clientId, clientSecret);
    var tokenResponse = await context.AcquireTokenAsync("https://management.azure.com/", clientCredential);
    var accessToken = tokenResponse.AccessToken;
    return accessToken;
}
```

Step 2: Create a Function App
After getting access token, you can create function app by making a PUT request to the following URL 

```PUT https://management.azure.com/subscriptions/{subscriptionId}/resourceGroups/{resourceGroupName}/providers/Microsoft.Web/sites/{name}?api-version=2018-02-01```

Find details here : https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/rest/api/appservice/webapps/createorupdate

Step 3: Deploy a Function to the Function App

After create function app, you can deploy your functions using function app deployment API.

```POST https://management.azure.com/subscriptions/{subscriptionId}/resourceGroups/{resourceGroupName}/providers/Microsoft.Web/sites/{name}/publishxml?api-version=2018-02-01```

Payload will include details like packageUri and others.

Find details here: https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/rest/api/appservice/webapps/publishzip

Make sure to replace {subscriptionId}, {resourceGroupName} and {name} with actual values. Also set "Authorization: Bearer {token}" in headers with actual access token received in step 1.

Please note that creating function app needs a service plan which also can be created using REST API. Follow similar process to create service plan first if you don't have one already. Then you can use that service plan id to create function app.

Also, REST API's are quite verbose and detailed. It requires considerable efforts to get all parameters and sequences correctly. If not necessary or if possible, consider using Azure Command Line Interface (CLI), It provides much simpler and consumable commands.

A common way to add AJAX to an ASP.NET MVC application with jQuery is to focus on partial views and JSON results. Your goal should be achieved if you write your controllers so that they return JSON data or partial views, according to the client request.

Here are the steps you can follow:

1. **Create your action method:** This action returns a ViewResult if the incoming request is not Ajax and PartialViewResult in case of Ajax request.

```csharp
public ActionResult ShowItem(int id)
{
   var item = _repository.GetItem(id);
   if (Request.IsAjaxRequest())   // AJAX Request
   {
      return PartialView("_ItemPartial", item);
   }
   else  // Normal HTTP Request
   {
      return View(item);   
   }
}
```

Here `_ItemPartial` is your partial view that only includes the markup and bindings for an item. If the request comes from AJAX, the controller action will only return the necessary markup and data. In case of a regular HTTP request, it'll return the complete view.

2. **Use jQuery AJAX to get the partial results:** Use a jQuery AJAX method to make an asynchronous request. In the `success` callback, replace the contents of some DOM element with the server response.

```javascript
$.ajax({
   url: '/Controller/ShowItem',
   type: 'GET',
   data: { id: 1 },
   success: function(result) {
      $('#itemContainer').html(result);
   }
});
```

3. **Use JSON Results**: Often, it's even more effective to return pure JSON data and do all the view changes in the client JavaScript. This minimizes the server load and can improve your app responsiveness. 

For that, just change your action method to:

```csharp
public JsonResult GetItem(int id)
{
   var item = _repository.GetItem(id);
   return Json(item, JsonRequestBehavior.AllowGet);
}
```

Your success function will also need to change:

```javascript
$.ajax({
   url: '/Controller/GetItem',
   type: 'GET',
   data: { id: 1 },
   dataType: 'json',
   success: function(item) {
      // Here you use item to update your view in the way you want
   }
});
```

Remember this approach requires some changes in your JavaScript to manually update your views, but it can be more flexible and efficient if you have complex views that only change partially with different data.

