In PascalABC.NET the syntax could look something like this:

```csharp
public Dictionary<string, int> FindSwapPairs(int[] sequence)
{
    int index = -1;
    int swapWith = -1;

    for(int i = sequence.Length - 1; i > 0; i--)
    {
        if(sequence[i] < sequence[i-1])
        {
            index = i;
            break;
        }
    }

    if(index != -1)
    {
        for(int i = index - 1; i >= 0; i--)
        {
            if(sequence[i] < sequence[index])
            {
                swapWith = i;
                break;
            }
        }
    }

    return new Dictionary<string, int>{{"index", index}, {"swap_with", swapWith}};
}
```

This method uses a for loop in reversed order, checking if the current item is less than the one before it. If it is, it saves the index and breaks the loop. If it found a lower item, it makes another reversed order loop from the found index backwards to find the next item that is less than the found item, saves it and breaks the loop. The two found indexes are returned in a Dictionary.

The problem is to sort an array in PascalABC.NET. The best sorting algorithm for this will be QuickSort because of its average-case time complexity of O(n log n). 

Here is a PascalABC.NET implementation of the Quick Sort algorithm:

```PascalABC.NET
using System;

public class SortArray {

    private static void QuickSort(int[] arr, int low, int high) {
        if (low < high) {

            int partitionIndex = Partition(arr, low, high);

            // recur for sub array
            QuickSort(arr, low, partitionIndex - 1);
            QuickSort(arr, partitionIndex + 1, high);
        }
    }

    static int Partition(int[] arr, int low, int high) {
        int pivot = arr[high];

        int temp = 0;
        int i = low - 1;
        for (int j = low; j < high; j++) {
            if (arr[j] < pivot) {
                i++;
                temp = arr[i];
                arr[i] = arr[j];
                arr[j] = temp;
            }
        }

        temp = arr[i+1];
        arr[i+1] = arr[high];
        arr[high] = temp;

        return i + 1;
    }

    static void PrintArray(int[] arr) {
        foreach (int value in arr) {
            Console.Write(value + " ");
        }
    }

    public static void Main() {
        int[] arr = {10, 7, 8, 9, 1, 5};
        int n = arr.Length;

        QuickSort(arr, 0, n-1);
        Console.WriteLine("Sorted array: ");
        PrintArray(arr);
     }
}
```

First, we have used the partition function, it takes an element as pivot, places the pivot element at its correct place in sorted array, and places all smaller to left of pivot and all greater elements to right of pivot.

The Main function creates an array and prints the sorted array.

And the QuickSort function takes an array and two indices, then it fixes the pivot element in its correct place (via calling Partition function), then it recursively calls itself for the sub-arrays to the left and right of the pivot element.

This code will consistently run with a time complexity of O(n log n), which is the optimal time complexity for a general purpose sort. The function also attempts to minimize swapping where possible.

However, do note that while quick sort is generally considered fast for large datasets, its worst-case time complexity is O(n^2), which can happen when the input array is already sorted. In such cases, other sorting algorithms may be more optimal.

