In general, it is a best practice not to throw exceptions in Dispose methods. Dispose is intended for cleanup of resources, and if exceptions are thrown during cleanup, they can potentially mask other important errors or cause problems with proper cleanup.

The reason Dispose might call Close() without a try-catch block is probably because Close() can throw exceptions that the code cannot or should not handle. For example, if Close() throws an exception because of an underlying network connection error, there's not much the Dispose method can do to handle this error.

Moreover, these exceptions are important for client to know that the connection has not closed correctly and client might need to take appropriate actions based upon types of exceptions.

What's usually done in such cases is the `try/finally` pattern: 

```csharp
public void Dispose()
{
    try
    {
        Close();
    }
    finally
    {
        // Cleanup
    }
}
```
or, `try/catch/throw` pattern to log the issue before escalating it:

```csharp
public void Dispose()
{
    try
    {
        Close();
    }
    catch (Exception ex)
    {
        // log the issue
        throw;
    }
}
```
This ensures that even if Close() throws an exception, you still have the chance to clean up other resources in the finally block. The exception still bubbles up the call stack, so you also don't mask any problems. 

Please note that catching all exceptions and swallowing them is generally a bad practice, because you can miss important error conditions or leave your program in an inconsistent state.  In case of IDisposable, a using block will catch exceptions and re-throw calling environment, allowing more robust exception handling when needed. 

But, `IDisposable.Dispose()` is not supposed to throw exceptions. If the cleanup code might throw exceptions, these exceptions can be caught and suppressed in the `Dispose()` method itself. That said, proper exception handling depends on the specific use case.

