Below is an example of how you could develop a pseudo-random number generator in PascalABC.NET. This class library uses the system's random number generator, which is inherently pseudo-random, meaning it generates numbers that appear to be random within certain constraints but are not truly random.

To ensure distinctness, it uses a HashSet, which automatically rejects duplicate values. If a generated number already exists in the HashSet, a new number is generated.

```PascalABC.NET
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class RandomNumberGenerator
{
    private Random _random;
    private HashSet<int> _numbers;

    public RandomNumberGenerator()
    {
        // Initializes the _random object with a seed that is time-dependent, 
        // for a unique sequence each time the generator is instantiated
        this._random = new Random();

        // Initializes the _numbers object which will store the generated sequence
        this._numbers = new HashSet<int>();
    }

    // Generates a distinct sequence of 10 numbers within the range 1-100
    public HashSet<int> GenerateSequence()
    {
        _numbers.Clear();

        while (_numbers.Count < 10)
        {
            int num = _random.Next(1, 101);

            if (!_numbers.Add(num))
            {
                // If a duplicate number is encountered, prompt a warning and continue
                Console.WriteLine("Warning: Attempted to insert a duplicate number into the sequence. Retrying...");
            }
        }

        return _numbers;
    }
}
```

This class can easily be exported to a DLL and used in other PascalABC.NET applications. Note that to truly remove any system clock influence, you would need to replace the default .NET random number generator with a custom true random number generator, which would require complex logic and potential usage of APIs.

