Unit testing `HttpModule` or any code that is tightly coupled with framework-specific classes like `HttpApplication` and `HttpContext` can be tricky because these classes do not have interfaces or virtual methods.

To unit test this kind of code, you can use a couple of different techniques:

1. **Abstracting/Wrapper Class Approach**: You can create an interface and a wrapper class that delegates to the actual `HttpContext` class. Your `HttpModule` would then only interact with the `HttpContext` through this wrapper, which could be mocked out in your tests.

```csharp
public interface IContextWrapper
{
    string GetRequestUrl();

    //...other necessary methods
}

public class HttpContextWrapper : IContextWrapper
{
    private readonly HttpContext _context;

    public HttpContextWrapper(HttpContext context)
    {
        _context = context;
    }

    public string GetRequestUrl()
    {
        return _context.Request?.Url?.ToString();
    }

    //...other necessary methods
}

```
Then in your tests, you can create a mock of `IContextWrapper`.

```csharp
[Test]
public void TestMethod_with_MockContext()
{
    var mockContext = new Mock<IContextWrapper>();
    mockContext.Setup(c => c.GetRequestUrl()).Returns("http://test.com");

    var module = new HttpModule(mockContext.Object);

    // Call method to test

    // Make assertions
}
```
2. **Use a Mocking Framework that can Mock Sealed/Non-Virtual Methods:** There are several .NET mocking libraries such as Moq, NSubstitute, and Rhino Mocks that only work with interfaces or virtual methods. But, there are others (like Typemock Isolator, JustMock, etc.) that can mock sealed classes or non-virtual methods. They often require a commercial license and run in an elevated mode (CLR profiler API, debug mode, etc.), which is why they can mock sealed/non-virtual classes.

Here is an example with Typemock:

```csharp
[Test, Isolated] // Isolated attribute is for TypeMock
public void TestMethod_with_TypeMockContext()
{
    var fakeContext = Isolate.Fake.Instance<HttpContext>();
    Isolate.WhenCalled(() => fakeContext.Request.Url).WillReturn(new Uri("http://test.com"));

    HttpModule module = new HttpModule();
    module.Init(fakeContext); // Hypothetically
    // Call method to test
    // Make assertions
}
```
3. **Backdoor for Testing:** In some cases, consider adding internal properties to `HttpModule` or the class where you use `HttpContext` that are only used for testing. This allows you to inject `HttpContext` or its properties and replace the actual implementations in testing.

Note: Each approach has trade-offs between simplicity, test isolation, maintainability, and performance. To choose the best is based on your exact needs and constraints.

