Yes, you can achieve redundancy and failover capabilities on a WCF service layer by having a cluster of WCF services and putting a load balancer in front of them. The load balancer will handle the primary and failover server configuration, and software-based load balancers like HAProxy and NGINX also handle failover situations by rerouting traffic to available servers.

Keep in mind, this setup itself is not a feature of WCF and there is no out-of-the-box solution from .NET/WCF to set a failover endpoint. However, in your client code you can use the ChannelFactory class to create proxy on the fly and switch to the alternative server. In this case, you will need to handle faults and retries. 

Here is simple example of how switching between endpoints could be implemented:

```csharp
List<EndpointAddress> endpoints = new List<EndpointAddress>
{
    new EndpointAddress("net.tcp://Server1/MyService"),
    new EndpointAddress("net.tcp://Server2/MyService")
};

foreach (var endpoint in endpoints)
{
    try
    {
        ChannelFactory<IMyService> factory = new ChannelFactory<IMyService>(myBinding, endpoint);
        IMyService proxy = factory.CreateChannel();
        proxy.MyServiceMethod();
        ((IClientChannel)proxy).Close();
        factory.Close();
    }
    catch (Exception)
    {
        // Handle exception or switch to next endpoint.
        continue; 
    }
    // No failover if operation is successful
    break;
}
```

In this example code, we first try to connect to Server1, and if it fails, we then try Server2.

Please note that this is a simple example and does not consider possible issues regarding timeouts or situations where the server becomes unavailable during the operation etc. 

