I'm trying to unit test a custom ConfigurationSection I've written, and I'd like to load some arbitrary configuration XML into a [System.Configuration.Configuration](http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/system.configuration.configuration.aspx) for each test (rather than put the test configuration xml in the Tests.dll.config file.  That is, I'd like to do something like this:


Configuration testConfig = new Configuration("<?xml version=\"1.0\"?><configuration>...</configuration>");
MyCustomConfigSection section = testConfig.GetSection("mycustomconfigsection");
Assert.That(section != null);



However, it looks like [ConfigurationManager](http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/system.configuration.configurationmanager.aspx) will only give you Configuration instances that are associated with an EXE file or a machine config.  Is there a way to load arbitrary XML into a Configuration instance?

answer:
Unfortunately, you cannot load arbitrary XML into a Configuration instance using the framework's built-in methods.

However, you can create a temporary file, write your XML configuration into it, instantiate a Configuration with this file, run your tests, and finally delete the temporary file. An example of how to do it is shown below:

```csharp
var tmpFileName = System.IO.Path.GetTempFileName();

try
{
    // write the arbitrary XML configuration into a new temp file
    File.WriteAllText(tmpFileName, arbitraryXmlConfiguration);

    System.Configuration.ExeConfigurationFileMap configMap = new ExeConfigurationFileMap();
    configMap.ExeConfigFilename = tmpFileName;

    System.Configuration.Configuration config = 
        ConfigurationManager.OpenMappedExeConfiguration(
            configMap, ConfigurationUserLevel.None);

    // Now you can use the 'config' object for your unit tests...
    MyCustomConfigSection section = (MyCustomConfigSection)config.GetSection("mycustomconfigsection");
    Assert.That(section != null);
}
finally
{
    // Do not forget to delete the temp file!
    File.Delete(tmpFileName);
}
```

The tmpFileName variable is the full path to a uniquely-named, zero-byte temporary file.
Please replace the "arbitraryXmlConfiguration" with your XML configuration. 

The Configuration instance is created by the call to ConfigurationManager.OpenMappedExeConfiguration passing the configuration file map to tell it where the temporary file is.

After you have done your tests, you must delete the temporary file. It is placed into the finally part of a try/finally block to ensure that the deleting operation happens whether an exception is thrown or not. 

As you can see, your XML configuration must be written into a file because of the nature of the .NET Configuration class and its related classes. They're designed to work with files, either the machine.config, the app.config or web.config files, or external configurartion files.

