The following Regex pattern uses the `Singleline` option which means the `.` (dot) captures all characters including new line (`\n`). It also considers case insensitivity, white-space and use of both double and single quotes around the HREF attribute:

```csharp
var matches = Regex.Matches(htmlContent, @"<a\s+(.*?href=['""](?<url>[^'""]*)['""])(.*?(?<label>(?<=\>).+?(?=\<\/a)))", RegexOptions.IgnoreCase | RegexOptions.Singleline);
foreach (Match match in matches)
{
    var url = match.Groups["url"].Value;
    var label = match.Groups["label"].Value;
    // process hyperlinks...
}
```
- `.*?href=['"](?<url>[^'""]*)['"]` will match `href` attribute and capture the URL into `url` named group.
- `.*?(?<label>(?<=\>).+?(?=\<\/a))` will match all the content between `>` and `</a>` tags and capture it into `label` named group.

The label may have other HTML tags inside (`<b>`, `<i>`, etc.), but they will be included into the match. If you want to remove them, you may use a HTML parser library or a regex replace for `<.*?>`. However, parsing HTML with regex is not recommended except for very simple cases, because HTML has a context (nested tags) that cannot be handled by regular expressions. For complex scenarios, consider using HTML parsers like HtmlAgilityPack or AngleSharp.

