The .NET Framework does not have any inbuilt method of checking the command line parameters of another running instance of an application. The .NET Framework only allows you to access the command line parameters for your own application, not others.

However, there are a few workarounds to achieve this:

1. Inter Process Communication (IPC): When your application starts, you can create a Named Pipe or Socket and send a message containing the command line parameters to the IPC mechanism. Any other instances of your application can then connect to the IPC mechanism and read out the command line parameters.

2. WMI: It's possible to do this with WMI (Windows Management Instrumentation). However, it only provides the ability to read the command-line parameters of another process on the same computer. It is not straightforward and WMI generally has permissions/security considerations as it allows for wide-ranging system access. 

