In PascalABC.NET, you can create an event observer by defining a custom delegate, attaching it to an event and raising the event whenever the state of the object changes. 

Here’s an illustrative example:

```csharp
using System;

// Custom delegate.
public delegate void StateChangedEventHandler(string message);

public class EventObserver
{
    // Custom event based on the delegate.
    public event StateChangedEventHandler StateChanged;

    private string _state;

    // State property
    public string State
    {
        get { return _state; }
        set
        {
            _state = value;
            // Raise event when state changes.
            OnStateChanged($"State has changed to: {_state}");
        }
    }

    // Method responsible for invoking the event.
    protected virtual void OnStateChanged(string message)
    {
        // Check to see if any subscribers.
        if (StateChanged != null)
        {
            // Call the event.
            StateChanged.Invoke(message);
        }
    }
}

public class Example
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        EventObserver observer = new EventObserver();

        // Subscribing to the event using a lambda expression.
        observer.StateChanged += message => Console.WriteLine(message);

        // Changing state - this will trigger the event.
        observer.State = "Standby";
        observer.State = "Active";
    }
}
```
This program is an example of the Observer pattern where `EventObserver` is the Subject (or Observable) and the function defined in the `Main` method is the Observer. The Subject's state, represented by the `_state` field, is being observed. Whenever the state changes, all subscribed Observers are notified.

Please note that `+=` allows the subscription of multiple observers to the `StateChanged` event. When the `StateChanged` event is invoked, all subscribed observers are notified. In this case, there is only one observer which simply writes the new state to the console. 

You should replace the console write line statement with appropriate code to react to the state change event.

