using System.Linq;

public class Program {
  public static void Main() {
    string hexValue = "ABCF";

    Random rnd = new Random();
    for(int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
      string result = rnd.Next(Convert.ToInt32(hexValue, 16)).ToString("X");
      Console.WriteLine("Hexadecimal: " + result);
  }
}
```
The code correctly generates a random sequence. However, ensure that revision incorporates:

1. A method to ensure that generated values are unique until all possible combinations have been exhausted.
2. Some form of error handling for when all combinations have been exhausted.
3. Reusability so the random hexadecimal generator could be utilized in different parts of a larger program. 

Bonus marks are given for:

1. Optimizing the function to reduce its runtime.
2. Utilizing software best practices.
3. Making the program thread safe.

answer:
Here's a revision of the provided code that includes all of the requested enhancements:
```PascalABC.NET
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Threading;

public class RandomHexGenerator
{
    private static HashSet<string> generatedStrings = new HashSet<string>();
    private static Random rnd = new Random();

    public static void Main()
    {
        string hexValue = "ABCF";
        int totalCount = Convert.ToInt32(hexValue, 16);

        for (int i = 0; i < totalCount; i++)
        {
            try
            {
                string result = GenerateUniqueHexValue(totalCount);
                Console.WriteLine("Hexadecimal: " + result);
            }
            catch (InvalidOperationException e)
            {
                Console.WriteLine(e.Message);
                return;
            }
        }
    }

    public static string GenerateUniqueHexValue(int maxValue)
    {
        string result;
        lock (rnd) 
        {
            result = rnd.Next(maxValue).ToString("X");

            if (!generatedStrings.Add(result))
            {
                if (generatedStrings.Count >= maxValue)
                {
                    throw new InvalidOperationException("All combinations have been exhausted.");
                }

                return GenerateUniqueHexValue(maxValue);
            }
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

In the updated version I've moved the the random value generation into a separate, reusable `GenerateUniqueHexValue` method. This method ensures that values are unique until all combinations have been exhausted, handling any errors that occur when this happens by throwing an exception. 

