In PascalABC.NET, you can write a function to determine whether a number is prime as follows. This program also includes error handling and edge-case evaluation:

```csharp
using System;

public class Program
{
    public static void main(String[] args)
    {
        try
        {
            Console.Write("Enter a positive integer: ");
            int number = Convert.ToInt32(Console.ReadLine());
            Console.WriteLine(IsPrime(number) ? $"{number} is a prime." : $"{number} is not a prime.");
        }
        catch (FormatException fe)
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Input is not a valid format.");
        }
        catch (OverflowException oe)
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Input is out of range for an Int32.");
        }
    }

    static bool IsPrime(int number)
    {
        if (number <= 1) return false;
        if (number == 2 || number == 3) return true;
        if (number % 2 == 0 || number % 3 == 0) return false;

        int i = 5;
        int w = 2;

        while (i * i <= number)
        {
            if (number % i == 0) return false;

            i += w;
            w = 6 - w; //This operation alternates the value of w between 2 and 4
        }

        return true;
    }
}
```
This code will accept an input from the user, validate whether it's an integer, and then determine whether it's a prime number with the `IsPrime()` function. Enter a positive integer to validate whether it is a prime number or not.

The `IsPrime()` function handles edge cases. If the number is less than or equal to one, it is not prime. If the number is two or three, it is prime. Beyond that, the function skips even numbers and multiples of 3 for efficiency because even numbers greater than 2 and numbers multiples of 3 cannot be prime. This is all done while checking that the number is within the Int32 range and in the valid format.

