Yes, this is a common misunderstanding about how Castle Windsor and similar IOC containers work. Even though an object has "transient" lifestyle, this actually means that a new instance will be served every time it's asked for. It does not mean that Castle will automatically release the object as soon as you're done with it.

The main reason for this is because Castle can't know when you're done with an object, it can't track all references to that object in your application. So, it holds on to every object it creates until you explicitly release it.

That's why it's generally a good practice to always release objects that you resolved from Castle as soon as you're done with them.

Here's an example of how you could do it:

```csharp
var yourService = container.Resolve<IYourService>();
try 
{
  yourService.DoSomething();
} 
finally 
{
  container.Release(yourService);
}
```

Another solution would be to switch to using per-web-request lifestyle for your web objects, which essentially means that Castle will automatically release all objects at the end of a web request.

Finally, you could implement a custom scope where you handle the release of the objects yourself. For example, you could have a scope that starts when a message comes in and ends when the processing of that message is done. This would allow you to control when your objects get released.

In any case, it's crucial to remember that service lifetimes (transient, singleton, per-web-request) and the release policy (when the container decides to release the instance) are two different concerns with different configuration options.

