Here is a simple bubble sort implemented in PascalABC.NET using generic types. This implementation should perform type inference based on the variables passed into it, which means it will work for any data types that have a valid comparison operator (like < and >).

Note: This is a basic example for learning purposes. In a real-world application, it's recommended to use built-in sorting methods for handling collections of data. This is both safer and more efficient, since these methods are likely implemented using quicksort or some other O(n log n) sorting method, while bubble sort is O(n^2).

```PascalABC.NET
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using NUnit.Framework;

public static class Sorter
{
    public static void Sort<T>(IList<T> list) where T : IComparable<T>
    {
        var itemCount = list.Count;
        for (var i = 0; i < itemCount; i++)
        {
            for (var j = 0; j < itemCount - 1; j++)
            {
                if (list[j].CompareTo(list[j + 1]) > 0)
                {
                    // Swapping
                    var temp = list[j];
                    list[j] = list[j + 1];
                    list[j + 1] = temp;
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```
