As you've already mentioned, the `HttpClient.Timeout` property sets the timeout for the entire response, not just the connection. Currently, there's no built-in mechanism in .net framework to set a connection-specific timeout.

However, to get around this, you might try a `CancellationToken` which you can cancel after a certain time period.

Here's an example:

```csharp
int timeout = 30000;
var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
HttpClient client = new HttpClient();

try {
    var response = await client.GetAsync("http://example.com", cts.Token);
    // Process response here...
}
catch (OperationCanceledException)
{
    if(cts.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        // A timeout occurred.
    }
} 
