About your last concern, HttpClient maintains a pool of connections, which means that once a connection is established to a particular server, it can be reused for multiple requests, which minimizes the overhead of establishing a new connection every time a request is sent. So, it doesn’t necessarily mean a new connection is established every time you make a new request, even though creating a HttpClient for each request is an anti-pattern.

Yes, it is possible to load two different versions of the same assembly into two different AppDomains within the same process using reflection. This is actually one of the primary uses of AppDomains. They have their own set of static variables and cannot directly call methods in each other. However, they can communicate by using remoting or by passing serializable arguments and return values. 

You can do this by:

1. Creating a new AppDomain.

2. Loading your assembly into each AppDomain.

3. Then create instances and invoke its methods using the `CreateInstanceAndUnwrap` method from the `AppDomain` class.

Remember to unload the AppDomains when done.

Here is a very basic example:

```csharp
// Create new AppDomains
AppDomain appDomain1 = AppDomain.CreateDomain("AD1");
AppDomain appDomain2 = AppDomain.CreateDomain("AD2");

// Load an assembly into the AppDomains
AssemblyName assemblyName = AssemblyName.GetAssemblyName("path/to/your/assembly.dll");
appDomain1.Load(assemblyName);
appDomain2.Load(assemblyName);

// Get a type from the assembly
Type yourType = assemblyName.GetExportedTypes().First(t => t.Name == "YourTypeName");

// Create instances of your type
var yourInstance1 = appDomain1.CreateInstanceAndUnwrap(assemblyName.FullName, yourType.FullName);
var yourInstance2 = appDomain2.CreateInstanceAndUnwrap(assemblyName.FullName, yourType.FullName);

// Call a method on your type
MethodInfo methodToCall = yourType.GetMethod("MethodName");
methodToCall.Invoke(yourInstance1, new object[] { })
methodToCall.Invoke(yourInstance2, new object[] { })

// Unload the AppDomains when done
AppDomain.Unload(appDomain1);
AppDomain.Unload(appDomain2);
```

