In PascalABC.NET, you can use the `System.IO.Directory` and `System.IO.DirectoryInfo` classes to search a directory for files that match a specified pattern.

Here is an example:

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        string startDirectory = @"c:\temp";

        string[] fileEntries = Directory.GetFiles(startDirectory, "*.txt"); // This line will search for all .txt files.

        foreach (string fileName in fileEntries)
        {
            Console.WriteLine(fileName); // This line will print the name of each .txt file in the specified directory.
        }

        // To retrieve subdirectories and their respective files.
        DirectoryInfo di = new DirectoryInfo(startDirectory);
        DirectoryInfo[] directories = di.GetDirectories();

        foreach (DirectoryInfo dir in directories)
        {
            fileEntries = Directory.GetFiles(dir.FullName, "*.txt"); // This line will search for all .txt files in each subdirectory.

            foreach (string fileName in fileEntries)
            {
                Console.WriteLine(fileName); // This line will print the name of each .txt file in each subdirectory.
            }
        }

        Console.ReadKey();
    }
}
```
In the above example, we are searching for all .txt files in a specified directory and its subdirectories.

The `GetFiles` method takes two parameters: `path` and `searchPattern`. The `path` parameter represents the directory to search for files inside of, and `searchPattern` parameter is the search string to match against the names of files in path. This parameter can take a combination of literal and wildcard characters (`*`, `?`).

If you want to search the directory and all its subdirectories in one go, you can use this line:

```csharp
string[] fileEntries = Directory.GetFiles(startDirectory, "*.txt", SearchOption.AllDirectories);
```
This will find all .txt files not only in the `startDirectory`, but also in all its subdirectories too.

