The User Account Control (UAC) is a security component in Microsoft Windows. UAC allows all users to log in as Standard users but can temporarily elevate their permissions so they can carry out administrative tasks. The purpose of this is to stop applications making changes to the OS without explicit permission from the user.

Simulating the ++ key sequence programmatically depends on the context. If you are building a UI automation application, it's usually done by sending the WM_KEYDOWN and WM_KEYUP messages for the '+' character twice in a row. However, I assume you may be asking how to programmatically trigger UAC prompt to elevate privileges for a certain operation. To do it, you usually need to involve the application manifest file that specifies required privilege level.

Sample code in PascalABC.NET:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Diagnostics;
using System.ComponentModel;

class ElevatedProcess
{
    public static void RunAsAdministrator(string file)
    {
        Process p = new Process();
        p.StartInfo.UseShellExecute = true;
        p.StartInfo.Verb = "runas"; // The "runas" verb triggers UAC elevation
        p.StartInfo.FileName = file;
        try
        {
            p.Start();
        }
        catch (Win32Exception ex)
        {
            if(ex.NativeErrorCode == 1223)
                Console.WriteLine("The operation was canceled by the user.");
            else
                throw;
        }
    }
}
```

Please note that this code sample doesn't really "simulate" the pressing ++ sequence part. It's focused on the "running some process with administrative privileges" part which we commonly associate with UAC dialogs.

UAC doesn't exist in Windows XP, so there's no equivalent way to do this in XP. Prior to the introduction of UAC, applications ran with the same permissions as the user who is currently logged in, which could be a potential security risk if that user is an administrator. If your application needs administrative rights, it's recommended to mention it in your documentation so users can run it with "Run As..." option and choose appropriate account.

Remember to include a proper application manifest that marks your application as an application that requires administrator privileges. User Account Control (UAC) in Vista and later enforces the manifest to be used to elevate the application process when it’s started.

Yes, it is advisable to use mock objects for I/O operation like reading a file using stream reader or similar operations. The primary reason is that you may not want your unit tests to have dependencies on the I/O system or external files. These dependencies can make your tests slower, more complex and possibly unreliable.

Using mock objects helps you to isolate the unit of code you are testing and make your tests more predictable and quicker. 

A mock object of a stream reader might have a pre-defined string it “reads” as if it is a file. You'd program that string into the mock object, the unit of code you're testing asks the mock for data, the mock sends back the pre-programmed string.

Here's a simple example of how you might use a mocking library (like Moq) to create a mock of StreamReader:

```csharp
var mockStreamReader = new Mock<StreamReader>();
mockStreamReader.Setup(sr => sr.ReadLine()).Returns("Mock line from file");

// To use the mocked StreamReader
var myService = new MyService(mockStreamReader.Object);
```
In this example, any calls to `StreamReader.ReadLine()` will return the string `"Mock line from file"`, meaning no actual file needs to be read.

Keep in mind, though, mocks can't and shouldn't be used to replace every kind of external dependency - sometimes, real integration tests (which do use actual I/O) might be necessary as well. Additionally, not all classes and methods can be mocked directly - they might need to be wrapped in a way that allows for creating mocks.

