```csharp
public string ReplaceVowels(string str)
{
    Dictionary<char, char> replacements = new Dictionary<char, char>()
    {
        {'a', 'e'},
        {'e', 'i'},
        {'i', 'o'},
        {'o', 'u'},
        {'u', 'a'},
        {'A', 'E'},
        {'E', 'I'},
        {'I', 'O'},
        {'O', 'U'},
        {'U', 'A'}
    };

    StringBuilder result = new StringBuilder(str.Length);

    foreach(char c in str)
    {
        if (replacements.ContainsKey(c))
            result.Append(replacements[c]);
        else
            result.Append(c);
    }

    return result.ToString();
}
```

This function works by iterating over every character in the input string and appending the replacement character into a StringBuilder if that character is a vowel. If it's not a vowel, the original character will be appended instead. After going over every character, the function will return the final string built by the StringBuilder.

The main advantage of using a Dictionary here is to have a constant time complexity O(1) for looking up a character's replacement vowel. Using a StringBuilder is beneficial compared to string concatenation operations that have a time complexity of O(n) every time a new string gets created.

Here's how to use the function:

