In order to avoid these exceptions, we can use the Invoke() or BeginInvoke() methods provided by all WinForms controls. These methods allow a delegate to be called on the same thread that the control was created on — providing a safe way to update UI controls from another thread.

Here is an example:

```csharp
private void button_Click(object sender, EventArgs e)
{
    Task.Factory.StartNew(() =>
    {
        // This is on a worker thread
        var result = DoSomeLongRunningTask();

        // If we try to update a UI control directly here, we would get an exception
        // textBoxResult.Text = result;

        // So instead, we use Invoke to run the update on the UI thread
        this.Invoke((Action)(() => textBoxResult.Text = result));
    });
}
```

In this example, we are starting a task on a worker thread when a button is clicked. If we try to update the `textBoxResult` control directly from the worker thread after the task is finished, we would get an exception.

So instead, we pass an Action to the `Invoke` method of the `textBoxResult` control, which will ensure that the action is performed on the UI thread, and won't throw an exception.

Also, note that `BeginInvoke` is a non-blocking asynchronous version that might be preferable in certain scenarios.

These principles are applicable for WinForms programming in PascalABC.NET. However, there might be similar thread dispatching mechanisms in other UI programming models as well. In WPF and UWP programming, `Dispatcher.Invoke() and Dispatcher.BeginInvoke()` are used, and in Android, the `runOnUiThread()` function serves a similar purpose.

