Develop a PascalABC.NET program to find the median value from a given array of integers. The array is generated from two already sorted arrays of integers. Bypass any sort function or method to find the median. The program must also handle erroneous or edge cases like when the two sorted arrays are of uneven length or if one of the arrays is empty. Additionally, consider optimization regarding time complexity of your solution.

Provided are pieces of code referencing the array of integers:

```PascalABC.NET
int[] nums1 = {1, 3, 4, 7, 10};
int[] nums2 = {2, 5, 6, 8, 11, 13};
```

And the erroneous code:

```PascalABC.NET
// This erroneous code simply finds the median of one array without
// considering that the array generated from two sorted arrays
public void FindMedianSortedArrays(int[] nums1, int[] nums2) {   
    int[] nums = nums1.Concat(nums2).ToArray();
    Array.Sort(nums);

    int median;
    if (nums.Length % 2 == 0)
        median = nums[nums.Length / 2];
    else
        median = nums[(nums.Length - 1) / 2];

    Console.WriteLine(median);    
}
```

Your task is to correct the above erroneous code to abide the given requirements.

answer:
From the provided problem, we are asked to find the median value from a given array of integers without using any sorting function. Also, the two arrays could have uneven lengths, and one or both of them could be empty. This problem can be efficiently solved using the merge process of the merge sort algorithm. Here's the corrected PascalABC.NET code:

```PascalABC.NET
public double FindMedianSortedArrays(int[] nums1, int[] nums2) {
    int x = nums1.Length, y = nums2.Length;
    if(x > y) return FindMedianSortedArrays(nums2, nums1); // Making sure nums2 is the bigger array

    int low = 0, high = x;
    while(low <= high) {
        int partitionX = (low + high)/2;
        int partitionY = (x + y + 1)/2 - partitionX;

        int maxLeftX = (partitionX == 0) ? Int32.MinValue : nums1[partitionX-1];
        int minRightX = (partitionX == x) ? Int32.MaxValue : nums1[partitionX];

        int maxLeftY = (partitionY == 0) ? Int32.MinValue : nums2[partitionY-1];
        int minRightY = (partitionY == y) ? Int32.MaxValue : nums2[partitionY];

        if(maxLeftX <= minRightY && maxLeftY <= minRightX) {
            if((x + y) % 2 == 0) 
                return ((double)Math.Max(maxLeftX, maxLeftY) + Math.Min(minRightX, minRightY))/2;
            else 
                return (double)Math.Max(maxLeftX, maxLeftY);
        } else if(maxLeftX > minRightY) {
            high = partitionX - 1;
        } else {
            low = partitionX + 1;
        }
    }

    throw new ArgumentException("Input arrays are not sorted");
}
```

This approach partitions the two arrays in such a way that elements on the left side are always smaller than elements on the right side. It keeps adjusting the partition until it meets the required condition for finding a median. Its time complexity is O(log(min(x,y)), where x and y are the sizes of the given arrays.
The function returns the median of the merged arrays and throws an exception if the input arrays are not sorted.

