I'm curious about the PascalABC.NET translation of this code:
def fibfab_sequence(n: int, a: int, b: int, c: int, d: int, e: int) -> int:
    fibfab_values = [a, b, c, d, e]
    for i in range(5, n+1):
        fibfab_values.append(sum(fibfab_values[-5:]))
    return fibfab_values[n]

answer:
Here is the PascalABC.NET version of the same code. It uses the same logic of storing the last 5 values but it uses a Queue to store them instead of a list/array. This way we keep dequeuing and enqueueing to keep the relevant 5 values:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

public class Program
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(FibfabSequence(10, 1, 1, 2, 3, 5));
    }

    public static int FibfabSequence(int n, int a, int b, int c, int d, int e)
    {
        Queue<int> queue = new Queue<int>();
        queue.Enqueue(a);
        queue.Enqueue(b);
        queue.Enqueue(c);
        queue.Enqueue(d);
        queue.Enqueue(e);

        for(int i = 5; i <= n; i++)
        {
            int sum = queue.Sum();
            queue.Dequeue();
            queue.Enqueue(sum);
        }

        return queue.Last();
    }
}
```

Note: This translated PascalABC.NET version is 0-based (0 to n towards the count of n+1). If you meant to be 1-based (1 to n for a count of n), you should adjust the "n" when calling the method.

