Here is a PascalABC.NET method to shuffle an array using a shuffle-seed (optional). 
This method uses System's `Random` to shuffle the elements in the array. When a shuffle-seed is provided, the Random object is initialized with the provided seed. This will ensure that the sequence of numbers produced by the Random object is always the same for the same seed, meaning the shuffled order will be the same for the same seed.

```csharp
using System;

public class Program
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        int[] array = new int[] { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 };
        Shuffle(array, 42);  // Using shuffle-seed = 42

        foreach(int i in array)
            Console.Write(i + " ");

        Console.WriteLine();

        Shuffle(array);  // Using no shuffle-seed

        foreach(int i in array)
            Console.Write(i + " ");
    }

    public static void Shuffle(int[] array, int? seed = null)
    {
        Random rnd = seed == null ? new Random() : new Random((int)seed);
        int n = array.Length;

        while(n > 1)
        {
            int k = rnd.Next(n--);
            int temp = array[n];
            array[n] = array[k];
            array[k] = temp;
        }
    }
}
```
In the `Main()` method, the array is shuffled twice: first with a seed of 42 and then without a seed. In the latter case, the `Random` object is initialized without a seed causing it to produce a completely random sequence of numbers. This will result in a completely random shuffle.

Note: Keep in mind that while this shuffle algorithm (known as the Fisher-Yates shuffle or the Knuth shuffle) produces a uniformly random permutation of the array, no random number generator is truly random. They are only pseudo-random. Therefore, even though the second shuffle (without a seed) should produce a different shuffle every time, it's theoretically possible to see the same shuffle if the random number generator happens to produce the same sequence of numbers.

