Here is a program to calculate LCM of two sets. The program expects two lists of integers and first calculates the LCM of the integers in each list (GetLCMOfNumbers function) and then calculates the LCM of the two results (GetLCM function). It has error handling for non-integer inputs.

```PascalABC.NET
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

class Program
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        try
        {
            List<int> numbers1 = new List<int>() {10, 15, 20};
            List<int> numbers2 = new List<int>() {25, 30, 35, 40};
            int lcm1 = GetLCMOfNumbers(numbers1);
            int lcm2 = GetLCMOfNumbers(numbers2);
            int finalLCM = GetLCM(lcm1, lcm2);
            Console.WriteLine("LCM of the two sets is: " + finalLCM);
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            Console.WriteLine(ex.Message);
        }
    }

    private static int GetLCMOfNumbers(List<int> numbers)
    {
        int lcm = numbers[0];
        for (int i = 1; i < numbers.Count; i++)
        {
            if (numbers[i] <= 0)
            {
                throw new ArgumentException("Input should be positive integer");
            }
            lcm = GetLCM(lcm, numbers[i]);
        }
        return lcm;
    }

    private static int GetLCM(int a, int b)
    {
        return a * b / GetGCD(a, b);
    }

    private static int GetGCD(int a, int b)
    {
        while (a != 0 && b != 0)
        {
            if (a > b)
                a %= b;
            else
                b %= a;
        }
        return a | b;
    }
}
```

In the Main function, we first define two lists of integers: numbers1 and numbers2. Then, for each list, we get the LCM by calling the GetLCMOfNumbers function. After that, we get the final LCM by calling the GetLCM function over lcm1 and lcm2. 

In our GetLCMOfNumbers function, the LCM is computed between the first number and the next subsequent numbers. We also check if the number is less than or equal to 0, if it is, an ArgumentException is thrown. 

The GetLCM function calculates the LCM of two integers by multiplying the two numbers and dividing the product by their Greatest Common Divisor (GCD). 

The GCD of like numbers is calculated using Euclid's algorithm in the GetGCD function.

