Unfortunately, you cannot unload an assembly if it is loaded directly into your main application domain. This is a limitation of the .NET Framework, irrespective of whether you're using PascalABC.NET or VB.NET or any other .NET language.

The only way to Unload an assembly from a .NET AppDomain is to load that assembly into a completely new AppDomain, use your objects in the new domain as long as you want, and then unload the entire AppDomain when you're done.

Here is a small sample code how you can load assembly into another AppDomain:

```csharp
AppDomain newDomain = AppDomain.CreateDomain("NewDomain");
try
{
    AssemblyName assemblyName = AssemblyName.GetAssemblyName(assemblyFilePath);
    Assembly assembly = newDomain.Load(assemblyName);
    // do your stuff  
}
finally
{
    AppDomain.Unload(newDomain);
}
```

In the code, `NewDomain` is a completely new AppDomain. After the assembly is loaded into it and you've used your objects, you can unload the entire appDomain, which will unload your assembly as a side effect.

Remember that when you create a new AppDomain, objects in one AppDomain can't directly reference objects in another AppDomain. If you want to use your objects across AppDomains, you'll need to use a technology such as .NET Remoting, which can get quite complex.

This is admittedly a bit messy, but it's necessary due to the way the CLR handles assemblies. It's also worth noting that in many cases, it's not necessary to unload an assembly. Assemblies are unloaded automatically when your application closes, and if you're simply trying to replace an existing assembly with an updated version, you can often do so without unloading the original assembly.

