Here is an additional idea for handling unhandled exceptions in a Remote Object.

With .Net Remoting, you can handle the exceptions thrown in the remote objects by extending RealProxy and TransparentProxy to weave some behavior around the call to the remote objects. In the invocation, you can setup a try-catch block to intercept all the unhandled exceptions.

Here's an example of how to use RealProxy to achieve this.

```CSharp
public class ExceptionHandlingRealProxy<T> : RealProxy
{
    private readonly T _decorated;

    public ExceptionHandlingRealProxy(T decorated)
        : base(typeof(T))
    {
        _decorated = decorated;
    }

    public override IMessage Invoke(IMessage msg)
    {
        IMethodCallMessage methodCall = (IMethodCallMessage)msg;
        IMethodReturnMessage methodReturn = null;

        try
        {
            object returnValue = methodCall.MethodBase.Invoke(_decorated, methodCall.InArgs);
            methodReturn = new ReturnMessage(returnValue, null, 0, methodCall.LogicalCallContext, methodCall);
        }
        catch (Exception e)
        {
            // Handle your Exception here and if required set to methodReturn
            // methodReturn = new ReturnMessage(e, methodCall);
            // Log your Exception
        }

        return methodReturn;
    }
}
```

You can create Proxy like this;

```CSharp
ExceptionHandlingRealProxy<YourClass> proxy = new ExceptionHandlingRealProxy<YourClass>(new YourClass());
```

Lastly, you use your object like this,

```CSharp
YourClass decorated = (YourClass)proxy.GetTransparentProxy();
```

From now on, when you call methods from the `decorated` instance, the call will be intercepted by the `Invoke` method. 

Remember that you need to recompile and redeploy your server application with these changes. If you are using third party remoting server then this method will not help. Be cautious before using this method and thoroughly test your application.

