Here's the equivalent PascalABC.NET code. 

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

public class Program
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(string.Join(",", PrimeProduct(100))); // 2, 5
        Console.WriteLine(string.Join(",", PrimeProduct(98))); // 2, 7
        Console.WriteLine(string.Join(",", PrimeProduct(17))); // 17
        Console.WriteLine(string.Join(",", PrimeProduct(111))); // 3, 37
        Console.WriteLine(string.Join(",", PrimeProduct(111111))); // 3, 7, 11, 13, 37
    }

    static bool IsPrime(int n)
    {
        if (n < 2) 
            return false;
        for (int i = 2; i <= Math.Sqrt(n); i++)
            if (n % i == 0) 
                return false;
        return true;
    }

    static List<int> PrimeProduct(int a)
    {
        List<int> factors = new List<int>();
        if (IsPrime(a))
            return new List<int> {a};
        for (int i = 2; i <= Math.Sqrt(a); i++)
        {
            if (a % i == 0 && IsPrime(i))
            {
                factors.Add(i);
                if (IsPrime(a / i))
                    factors.Add(a / i);
            }
        }
        factors = factors.Distinct().OrderBy(n => n).ToList();
        if(factors.Count > 3)
            throw new Exception("Not a product of unique prime numbers.");
        return factors;
    }
}
```

Explanation:

Here's a breakdown of the PascalABC.NET version:
* The IsPrime method is just like the Python method, only the for-loop uses `i <= Math.Sqrt(n)` instead of the `range(2, int(n ** 0.5) + 1)` in Python. Math.Sqrt(n) returns a double so we take the floor of it which is equivalent to converting double to int in PascalABC.NET.
* Also, Python uses `n % i == 0` to test for divisibility, where `%` is the modulus operator. PascalABC.NET uses the same operator for the same purpose.
* PrimeProduct method in PascalABC.NET also has the same structure as the Python function. Instead of a list in Python, we have a List<int> in PascalABC.NET. We also use the `Add` method instead of append, and `Distinct().OrderBy(n => n).ToList()` instead of `sorted(list(set(factors)))`.
* If the number of prime factors is more than 3, an exception is thrown in PascalABC.NET. I chose to throw an exception because it seems to best match the "Not a product of unique prime numbers" string in Python, although it's up to interpretation how you want to handle this case.
* We use `Console.WriteLine(string.Join(",", PrimeProduct(98)));` to print out the result. The `string.Join(",", PrimeProduct(98))` is equivalent to Python's `print(prime_product(98))`. The differences are that PascalABC.NET uses `WriteLine` and that `string.Join(",", PrimeProduct(98))` is used to concatenate list elements with a delimiter.

