I've got a generic<> function that takes a linq query ('items') and enumerates through it adding additional properties. How can I select all the properties of the original 'item' rather than the item itself (as the code below does)?

So equivalent to the sql: select *, 'bar' as Foo from items


foreach (var item in items)
{
    var newItem = new {
        item, // I'd like just the properties here, not the 'item' object!
        Foo = "bar"
    };

    newItems.Add(newItem);
}

answer:
In PascalABC.NET, anonymous types and object initializers go hand-in-hand. If you want to use an anonymous type to copy ‘items’ and to add a new property ('Foo') to it, unfortunately it is not possible to directly return a new anonymous type object that has the properties of a defined class (ex: 'item') and also new additional properties. The syntax you've tried does not do what you want, it will create a nested instance:

This behavior is nested because PascalABC.NET is a statically typed language, which means that type checking occurs at compile time. Therefore, the layout of the anonymous types and object initializers must be known at compile time.

As a workaround, if you know the type of 'item' and the type does not change frequently, you could explicitly select every property of 'item'. However, if 'item' has a dynamic type or if its type frequently changes, this will not be a good solution.

Here is a workaround for your situation:

```csharp
foreach (var item in items)
{
    var newItem = new {
        item.Property1, 
        item.Property2, 
        // Repeat above line for all properties of item
        Foo = "bar"
    };

    newItems.Add(newItem);
}
```

Another workaround would be using the ExpandoObject which can be used to create objects that have members dynamically added and removed. One downside to this, is that the resulting type is not anonymous, but dynamic.

```csharp
foreach (var item in items)
{
    IDictionary<string, object> newItem = new ExpandoObject();

    foreach(PropertyDescriptor propertyDescriptor in TypeDescriptor.GetProperties(item))
    {
        var obj = propertyDescriptor.GetValue(item);
        newItem.Add(propertyDescriptor.Name, obj);
    }

    newItem.Add("Foo", "bar");

    newItems.Add(newItem);
}
```

This approach will not work if you are doing this in a Linq to Entities query or any other kind of Linq provider because ExpandoObject does not translate to SQL.

Just keep in mind, if the type of 'item' changes frequently, maintaining this code will become more difficult over time. Which is why it's always a good idea, if possible, to design your code in such a way that selecting anonymous types and copying objects from known types is easier.

If you're dealing with the objects of the same class and you want to select all properties dynamically along with adding new properties, I'd suggest implementing the Clone or Copy method if applicable.

