For your requirements, you should consider using HashSet<T> that is provided in .NET 3.5 and later versions. It represents a set of values and provides high performance set operations like union, intersection etc. It works on basis of the hash-codes of the items, therefore the larger the amount of objects the more gain it provides over List<T> or T[].

HashSet is unordered but allows for the standard adding, removing and checking for existing elements. 

Also, you can convert between lists and hashsets rather efficiently:

```csharp
List<int> list = new List<int> {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
HashSet<int> hashSet = new HashSet<int>(list);

// And back again
list = hashSet.ToList();
```

A HashSet is a classic trade-off efficiency vs memory. It uses more memory, to reduce the time needed to look up elements. If you're working with a machine that has a low amount of RAM and your sets contain large objects, this might be a limiting factor.

Another option you might consider is SortedSet<T> provides a self-balancing tree which maintains itself in sorted order. It supports add, remove, and search operations in roughly O(log N) time where N is the number of elements. So, its performance is between that of a List<T> and a HashSet<T>. However, each of these collections excel in different types of operations, so you would need to do some benchmarking to find out which would perform best given the distribution of operations your application performs.

If you need to keep your sets sorted, then HashSet<T> is not the way to go because HashSet<T> does not guarantee any particular order of elements. You can use SortedSet<T> in that case. It represents a collection of objects that is maintained in sorted order with no duplicate elements. It supports add, remove, and search operations, just like HashSet<T>, but it also automatically sorts elements as they are added to the collection.

