Before we begin, it is important to note that recursion may not be the best solution when it comes to generating prime numbers for efficiency considerations. However, as the question asks for a recursive solution, we shall provide one.

Here's how you can define a recursive function in PascalABC.NET to generate all prime numbers in the range from 1 to 100:

```csharp
class Program
{
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        PrintPrimeNumbers(2, 100);
    }

    static void PrintPrimeNumbers(int current, int end)
    {
        if (current <= end)
        {
            if (IsPrime(current, 2))
            {
                Console.WriteLine(current);
            }

            PrintPrimeNumbers(current + 1, end);
        }
    }

    static bool IsPrime(int number, int divisor)
    {
        if (divisor >= number)
        {
            return true;
        }
        else if (number % divisor == 0)
        {
            return false;
        }
        else
        {
            return IsPrime(number, divisor + 1);
        }
    }
}
```
This program prints out all the prime numbers between 2 and 100. The `IsPrime` method checks if a number is prime by checking if it is divisible by any number up to itself. If the number is divisible, it is not a prime number and the method returns false. If not, it recursively calls itself to check the next number.

The `PrintPrimeNumbers` method is the one that establishes the range. It starts from 2 (as 1 is not a prime number) and ends at 100 (or whatever upper limit you want to set). It checks if the current number is prime, and if so, it prints it. Then it recursively calls itself to check the next number until it reaches the end of the range.

Again, this isn't the most efficient algorithm to find prime numbers, but it fulfills your request for a recursive solution in PascalABC.NET.

