This syntax is called "ValueTuples" and it is introduced in PascalABC.NET 7. It is an alternative to the traditional tuple which provides better syntax and performance.

1. This syntax is called ValueTuple declaration or ValueTuple syntax. ValueTuples, unlike Tuples, are value types (structs) not reference types (classes). This means they offer better performance because they avoid heap allocations.

2. The real world use case of ValueTuples is mainly returning multiple values from a method without having to create a class or struct to hold that data. This makes your code more concise and readable. The ability to name the elements in a ValueTuple makes it much clearer what the data represents, without having to read the context or documentation of the method.

For example, suppose you have a method that calculates the min and max value from a sequence. Instead of returning an array or a predefined class/struct with 2 elements, you can return a ValueTuple like this:

```csharp
public (int Min, int Max) CalculateMinMax(IEnumerable<int> sequence)
{
    var min = int.MaxValue;
    var max = int.MinValue;
    foreach (var number in sequence)
    {
        min = Math.Min(min, number);
        max = Math.Max(max, number);
    }
    return (min, max);
}
```

Then, when you call that method, it's immediately clear what the returned values represent:

```csharp
var (min, max) = CalculateMinMax(new[] {3, 5, -2, 8, 7});
