```csharp
using System;

public class InterestCalculator
{
    public decimal SimpleInterest(decimal principal, decimal rate, decimal time)
    {
        if (principal < 0 || rate < 0 || time < 0)
            throw new ArgumentException("Principal, rate or time cannot be negative");
        return (principal*rate*time) / 100;
    }

    public decimal CompoundInterest(decimal principal, decimal rate, decimal time, decimal compoundsPerYear)
    {
        if (principal < 0 || rate < 0 || time < 0 || compoundsPerYear <= 0)
            throw new ArgumentException("Principal, rate, time, or compounds per year cannot be negative or zero.");
        decimal powers = compoundsPerYear * time;
        decimal body = 1 + rate / compoundsPerYear / 100;

        return principal * (Decimal)Math.Pow((double)body, (double)powers) - principal;
    }

    public decimal ContinousCompoundInterest(decimal principal, decimal rate, decimal time)
    {
        if (principal < 0 || rate < 0 || time < 0)
            throw new ArgumentException("Principal, rate or time cannot be negative");
        return principal * ((decimal)Math.Pow(Math.E, (double)((rate * time) / 100)) - 1);
    }

    public decimal IrregularCompoundInterest(decimal principal, decimal rate, IEnumerable<decimal> times)
    {
        if (principal < 0 || rate < 0)
            throw new ArgumentException("Principal or rate cannot be negative");
        foreach(var time in times)
        {
            if (time < 0)
                throw new ArgumentException("Times cannot be negative");
            principal += CompoundInterest(principal, rate, time, 1);
        }

        return principal;
    }
}
```

Here we have a class `InterestCalculator` with four methods to calculate different types of interest:

- `SimpleInterest` calculates the simple interest using the formula (P*R*T)/100
- `CompoundInterest` calculates the compound interest using the formula P(1 + R/N)^(NT) - P
- `ContinousCompoundInterest` calculates the continuously compounded interest using the formula Pe^(RT) - P
- `IrregularCompoundInterest` calculates the irregularly compounded interest by repeatedly applying the compound interest formula for each time period

In each method, an `ArgumentException` is thrown if any of the input parameters are negative. In `CompoundInterest`, an additional check is made to ensure the `compoundsPerYear` is not zero or negative, as this wouldn't make sense in a compound interest calculation. 

In `IrregularCompoundInterest`, each time period is checked to ensure it is not negative before the compound interest is calculated. This approach ensures that the principal is not wrongly adjusted if an error is encountered during the compound interest calculation. 

All the exceptions throw a descriptive error message which should aid in debugging if an error occurs.

