Which mocking framework are you using? It would be helpful to know to provide a more specific solution. 

However, here's a generic guideline for setting up such a test using Moq. The same concept can be applied to other mocking frameworks like Rhino Mocks or NSubstitute. 

Assuming you have an interface like this:

```csharp
public interface IFoo
{
    void Bar1();
    void Bar2();
    void Bar3();
}
```

...then you could write a test like this:

```csharp
[Fact]
public void CallsAtLeastOneMethod()
{
    // Arrange
    var methodsCalled = new ConcurrentBag<string>();

    var mock = new Mock<IFoo>();
    mock.Setup(m => m.Bar1()).Callback(() => methodsCalled.Add("Bar1"));
    mock.Setup(m => m.Bar2()).Callback(() => methodsCalled.Add("Bar2"));
    mock.Setup(m => m.Bar3()).Callback(() => methodsCalled.Add("Bar3"));

    var serviceUnderTest = new ServiceUnderTest(mock.Object);

    // Act
    serviceUnderTest.DoSomething();

    // Assert
    methodsCalled.Count.Should().BeGreaterOrEqualTo(1);
}
```

In this test, we track the methods called by adding the method name to a `ConcurrentBag<string>` (a thread-safe list) whenever the method is called. After we call `DoSomething()` on the service under test, we check that the bag contains at least one method name.

As for Rhino Mocks, unfortunately, it does not provide the concept similar to `Callback` in Moq. All methods are expected to be called by default and your test will fail if any of them are not called. The best possible option is to have separate tests for each method. It's also a good practice to have separate tests for each method in your production code, as the single responsibility principle.

Take into account that this assumes your system under test calls one of the three methods. If it doesn't, the test will fail. This won't be able to verify that exactly one call was made. They might all be called, or two of them, but as long as at least one is called, the test will succeed. 

I hope this helps. If you're using a different mocking framework, please provide additional information.

