In PascalABC.NET, delegates are reference types used to encapsulate methods with similar signatures. An event is a special kind of delegate which is used when a class wants to notify other classes when something happens.

Here is a simple example:

```PascalABC.NET
using System;

// Declare a delegate type for an event 
public delegate void MyEventHandler(string message);

public class EventPublisher {
    // Declare an event based on that delegate
    public event MyEventHandler MyEvent;

    public void DoSomething() {
        // Do something that causes the event to fire
        OnMyEvent("Event happened");
    }

    protected virtual void OnMyEvent(string message) {
        // Fire the event - call the delegate
        MyEvent?.Invoke(message);
    }
}

public class EventSubscriber {
    public EventSubscriber(EventPublisher pub) {
        // Attach method to the event
        pub.MyEvent += HandleEvent;
    }

    // This method should have same signature as the delegate
    private void HandleEvent(string message) {
        Console.WriteLine("Something has happened: " + message);
    }
}

class Program {
    static void Main(string[] args) {
        EventPublisher pub = new EventPublisher();
        EventSubscriber sub = new EventSubscriber(pub);

        pub.DoSomething(); // will cause event to be fired, and event handler to run
    }
}
```
In the above code, `EventPublisher` class declares a delegate `MyEventHandler` and an event `MyEvent`. When the method `DoSomething()` is called, it subsequently calls `OnMyEvent()` method which then fires the `MyEvent`. The `EventSubscriber` class then registers its `HandleEvent()` method with the `MyEvent` of the `EventPublisher` instance such that when the event is fired, it prints a message to the console.

Here is the PascalABC.NET equivalent and explanation for the Python code you've provided:

```PascalABC.NET
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution
{
    public Tuple<double, double> MedianAndStandardDeviation(List<int> l)
    {
        // It represents Max Heap
        var lower = new SortedSet<Tuple<int, int>>();

        // It represents Min Heap
        var higher = new SortedSet<Tuple<int, int>>();

        var medians = new List<double>();
        var count = 0;

        foreach (var num in l)
        {
            var newEntry = new Tuple<int, int>(num, count++);

            // Add the first element (or) when a current number is smaller than max-heap root
            if (lower.Count == 0 || num < lower.Max.Item1)
                lower.Add(newEntry);
            else
                higher.Add(newEntry);

            // Balance the heaps
            if (lower.Count > higher.Count + 1)
            {
                var movedNum = lower.Max;
                lower.Remove(movedNum);
                higher.Add(movedNum);
            }
            else if (higher.Count > lower.Count)
            {
                var movedNum = higher.Min;
                higher.Remove(movedNum);
                lower.Add(movedNum);
            }

            // Calculate the median
            double median;
            if (lower.Count == higher.Count)
                median = (lower.Max.Item1 + higher.Min.Item1) / 2.0;
            else
                median = lower.Max.Item1;

            medians.Add(median);
        }

        // Calculate Standard Deviation
        var stdDev = CalculateStandardDeviation(medians);

        return new Tuple<double, double>(medians[^1], stdDev);
    }

    private double CalculateStandardDeviation(List<double> values)
    {
        double avg = values.Average();
        return Math.Sqrt(values.Average(v => Math.Pow(v - avg, 2)));
    }
}
```
- This PascalABC.NET code defines a class named 'Solution' and a method named 'MedianAndStandardDeviation' that calculates the median and standard deviation for a given list of integers as inputs.

- We use SortedSet for lower (Max Heap) and higher (Min Heap). SortedSet always keeps its elements sorted and it gives the flexibility to get Max and Min in O(1) time complexity. Each element in SortedSet is Tuple where Item1 is the actual number and Item2 is unique index to handle duplicate numbers as SortedSet does not allow duplicates.

- The foreach loop iterates through all numbers. Depending upon the number, it either pushes it into 'lower' or 'higher'. The heaps are then balanced.

- The median value is determined based on the count of the numbers processed so far. The calculated median is then added to the 'medians' list.

- CalculateStandardDeviation method is used to calculate the standard deviation of medians.

- The method returns the final calculated median and standard deviation as a Tuple.

Remember to use 'System' and 'System.Collections.Generic' namespaces for this function to work properly.

