answer:
XML Serializer doesn't just serialize object data, it also includes type information so that the objects can be re-created when they're de-serialized. It doesn't handle serialization for all types, it cannot serialize `Type` objects because technically these represent the metadata, not the serializable data.

However, if your goal is just to serialize the full type name and re-create the Type based on this information later, you could surround this with your own simple class, like so:

```csharp
public class SerializableType
{
    public string TypeName { get; set; }

    public SerializableType()
    {
    }

    public SerializableType(Type t)
    {
        TypeName = t.AssemblyQualifiedName;
    }

    public Type ToType()
    {
        return Type.GetType(TypeName);
    }
}
```

Here's how you can use it:

```csharp
Type myType = typeof(StringBuilder);
var serializableType = new SerializableType(myType);
var serializer = new XmlSerializer(typeof(SerializableType));
TextWriter writer = new StringWriter();
serializer.Serialize(writer, serializableType);
```

Remember, this won't serialize the metadata of the `Type`, it will only remember the type name (including namespace and assembly) so you can retrieve the `Type` object back. All the actual metadata will come from the .NET framework when you re-create your `Type` using `Type.GetType()`.

Here is a sample PascalABC.NET class that uses a binary search algorithm to find the target element in a sorted 2D array:

```csharp
public class MatrixSearcher
{
    public bool SearchMatrix(int[,] matrix, int target)
    {
        if (matrix == null || matrix.Length == 0)
            return false;

        int rows = matrix.GetLength(0);
        int columns = matrix.GetLength(1);

        int left = 0;
        int right = rows * columns - 1;

        while (left <= right)
        {
            int middle = left + ((right - left) / 2);
            int middleElementValue = matrix[middle / columns, middle % columns];

            if (middleElementValue == target)
                return true;
            else if (middleElementValue < target)
                left = middle + 1;
            else
                right = middle - 1;
        }

        return false;
    }
}
```

Understanding the Code:

- The binary search algorithm in this code is implemented through a while loop.
- The boundaries of the 2D array are checked by calculating the index in the form of rows and columns.
- The binary search algorithm continuously eliminates half of the elements being studied by comparing the middle element of the search space with the target element. If the target element is found, the function returns true.
- The optimization technique used here is the reduction of the search space by half after each iteration.
- The boundary handling is done by checking the left and right boundary index. It ensures we don't go beyond the matrix size while doing the binary search.
- Time complexity of this approach is O(log(m*n)), which is much better than the naive approach where we check every element in the matrix which would have resulted in O(m*n), where m represents the number of rows and n represents number of columns in the matrix.

