Devise an iterative algorithm in PascalABC.NET, utilising the principles of recursion and inferences from the Sieve of Eratosthenes, to display all unique, natural numbers greater than 1, which predominantly have only two distinct positive divisors, 1 and the number itself, in a custom numerical range.

Ensure your algorithm is efficient in terms of time and space complexity, handling larger ranges such as 1 to 1000000. 

Furthermore, as a programmer it is equally essential to debug code. Below, find a faulty implementation which uses an imperative approach instead of recursion but fails to provide the intended output. 

Understand and explain the defect in this code and refactor it to meet the above requirements. 

```
using System;  
public class Exercise21 
{  
    public static void Main() 
    {
        int n, m;
        Console.WriteLine(" The prime numbers  from 1 to 100 are: ");
        for(m=1; m<=100; m++)
        {
            int counter = 0;
            for(n=2; n<=m/2; n++)
            {
                if(m%n==0)
                {
                    counter++;
                    break;
                }
            }
            if(counter==0 && m!=1)
            Console.Write("{0} ",m);
        }
    Console.Write("\n\n");
    }
}
```

answer:
The faulty part of the code is in the "if" statement where it checks if `counter==0` and `m!=1`. The check `m!=1` is unnecessary due to the loop range and also it will mislead users. We need to change these checks now. The checking should start from `m=2` since `m=1` is not a prime number by definition. Instead of checking until `m/2`, checking up to the square root of `m` will be enough because a larger factor of the number should be a multiple of the smaller factor that has already been checked. 

The refactored and fixed code below also uses principle of Sieve of Eratosthenes :

```csharp
using System;
public class Exercise22
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.Write("Enter the upper limit: ");
        int upperLimit = Convert.ToInt32(Console.ReadLine());
        bool[] sieve = new bool[upperLimit + 1];
        for(int m = 2; m*m <= upperLimit; m++)
        {
            if(!sieve[m])
            {
                for(int n = m*m; n <= upperLimit; n += m)
                {
                    sieve[n] = true;
                }
            }
        }
        Console.WriteLine("Prime numbers from 1 to " + upperLimit + " are:");
        for(int i = 2; i <= upperLimit; i++)
        {
            if (!sieve[i])
            {
                Console.Write("{0} ", i);
            }
        }
        Console.Write("\n\n");
    }
}
```

This code first initializes an array of boolean values representing numbers up to the given maximum. Initially, every entry in the array is set to false, representing that every number is potentially a prime number.
Next, the code does a marking process similar to the Sieve of Eratosthenes. For every unmarked number m, iterate over the array and mark the multiples of m (except m itself) as non-prime (i.e., true in the sieve array). After completing this process, the primes are the numbers which are still left unmarked.
Finally, iterate over the array and print the numbers that are unmarked (prime numbers). The time complexity of the Sieve of Eratosthenes is O(n log(log n)).
The refactored code correctly finds all prime numbers up to the given maximum and is more efficient than the original code.

