You don't provide a specific erroneous code examples, but I will give you an example of a Fibonacci search algorithm implemented in PascalABC.NET. This algorithm works efficiently on larger size arrays with sequential access, for example multi-dimensional arrays or linked lists.

In Fibonacci search, we divide the array into two parts that do not have equal elements. The dividing line is the index stored in the Fibonacci number. This technique uses Fibonacci numbers, an increase which is incredibly fast, and allows this method to work faster than binary search algorithm for large sized arrays.

Below is an example of the Fibonacci search algorithm in PascalABC.NET:

```csharp
using System;

public class FibonacciSearch
{
    public static int Min(int x, int y)
    {
        return (x <= y) ? x : y;
    }

    public static int FibonacciSearchFunc(int[] arr, int x, int n)
    {
        int fibMMm2 = 0;
        int fibMMm1 = 1;
        int fibM = fibMMm2 + fibMMm1;

        while (fibM < n)
        {
            fibMMm2 = fibMMm1;
            fibMMm1 = fibM;
            fibM = fibMMm2 + fibMMm1;
        }

        int offset = -1;

        while (fibM > 1)
        {
            int i = Min(offset + fibMMm2, n - 1);
            if (arr[i] < x)
            {
                fibM = fibMMm1;
                fibMMm1 = fibMMm2;
                fibMMm2 = fibM - fibMMm1;
                offset = i;
            }
            else if (arr[i] > x)
            {
                fibM = fibMMm2;
                fibMMm1 = fibMMm1 - fibMMm2;
                fibMMm2 = fibM - fibMMm1;
            }
            else return i;
        }

        if(fibMMm1 == 1 && arr[offset + 1] == x)
            return offset + 1;

        return -1;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        int[] arr = {10, 22, 35, 40, 45, 50, 80, 82, 
                     85, 90, 100};
        int n = 11;
        int x = 85;
        Console.Write("Found at index: "+FibonacciSearchFunc(arr, x, n));
    }
}
``` 

This PascalABC.NET program defines a method called `FibonacciSearchFunc` that performs the Fibonacci search on the input array `arr[]`. Initially `fibM`, `fibMMm1` (Fibonacci minus 1) and `fibMMm2` (Fibonacci minus 2) are initialized to 1 and 0, respectively. Then the `while` loop inside `FibonacciSearchFunc` finds the smallest Fibonacci number greater than or equal to `n`, the size of the array. 

The rest of the code deals with moving the Fibonacci pointers (decreasing the search space) based on whether `x`, the element to be searched, is in the left or right part of the array split. 

Finally, if `fibMMm1` is 1 and the element just following `offset` is `x`, then `x` is found and its position is returned. If `x` is not found, then `-1` is returned.

Always please note that your array should be sorted before using the Fibonacci search. If the array is not sorted, the Fibonacci search algorithm will not work properly.

Also, this algorithm does not guarantee searching in constant time, as binary search does for arrays. The worst case time complexity can be up to O(log n). This is due to the fact that we perform the lookup of the element in the array by comparing it with the element at the Fibonacci index, which can be up to log n steps away from the target element.

