Sure, here is a simple example of how you might define and use a custom command in WPF using command bindings and input bindings.

Firstly, you will need to define your custom command. This is typically done as a static member of a class:

```csharp
public static class CustomCommands
{
    public static readonly RoutedUICommand MyCustomCommand = new RoutedUICommand
        (
            "MyCustomCommand",
            "MyCustomCommand",
            typeof(CustomCommands),
            new InputGestureCollection()
                {
                    new KeyGesture(Key.M, ModifierKeys.Control)
                }
        );
}
```

In the above code, I define a new command `MyCustomCommand` which is associated with the shortcut Ctrl+M.

Then, we need to bind this command, so that when it is invoked, it triggers a specific method:

```csharp
public partial class MainWindow : Window
{
    public MainWindow()
    {
        InitializeComponent();

        // Binding command with related functions
        CommandBinding commandBinding = new CommandBinding
        (
            CustomCommands.MyCustomCommand, 
            MyCustomCommand_Executed, 
            MyCustomCommand_CanExecute
        );
        this.CommandBindings.Add(commandBinding);
    }

    private void MyCustomCommand_Executed(object sender, ExecutedRoutedEventArgs e)
    {
        MessageBox.Show("MyCustomCommand Executed");
        // Your logic here
    }

    private void MyCustomCommand_CanExecute(object sender, CanExecuteRoutedEventArgs e)
    {
        // Your logic here
        e.CanExecute = true; // or your custom logic to check if command can be executed
    }
}
```

In this code, when the command is invoked, `MyCustomCommand_Executed` is called, where you would put your desired logic. There is also the `MyCustomCommand_CanExecute` method which defines whether or not the command can currently be executed.

Lastly, you use the command in your XAML code like so:
```xaml
