Remember that every version of Project Server has a lifecycle of 10 years, so Project Server 2003's lifecycle ended a long while ago, in 2014. Hence, it is integral to upgrade to ensure continued official support and improved functionality.

Here is a simple user-defined PascalABC.NET function that calculates both real and complex roots of a second-order polynomial, while also handling inputs in scientific notation:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Numerics;

public class QuadraticRoot
{
    public static void CalculateRoots(string a, string b, string c)
    {
        // Parsing the input coefficients in scientific notation
        double a_double = double.Parse(a, System.Globalization.NumberStyles.Float);
        double b_double = double.Parse(b, System.Globalization.NumberStyles.Float);
        double c_double = double.Parse(c, System.Globalization.NumberStyles.Float);

        // Calculating the discriminant
        double discriminant = b_double * b_double - 4 * a_double * c_double;

        if (discriminant > 0)
        {
            // Real and different roots
            double root1 = (-b_double + Math.Sqrt(discriminant)) / (2 * a_double);
            double root2 = (-b_double - Math.Sqrt(discriminant)) / (2 * a_double);

            Console.WriteLine("Roots are real and different.");
            Console.WriteLine("Root 1 = " + root1);
            Console.WriteLine("Root 2 = " + root2);
        }
        else if (discriminant == 0)
        {
            // Real and equal roots
            double root1 = -b_double / (2 * a_double);
            Console.WriteLine("Roots are real and same.");
            Console.WriteLine("Root 1 = Root 2 = " + root1);
        }
        else
        {
            // Complex roots
            double realPart = -b_double / (2 * a_double);
            double imaginaryPart = Math.Sqrt(-discriminant) / (2 * a_double);
            Console.WriteLine("Roots are complex and different.");
            Console.WriteLine("Root 1 = " + new Complex(realPart, imaginaryPart));
            Console.WriteLine("Root 2 = " + new Complex(realPart, -imaginaryPart));
        }
    }
}
```

You can call `CalculateRoots` function with any input in scientific notation. For example:

```csharp
QuadraticRoot.CalculateRoots("1E-2", "1E1", "2.5E2");
``` 

Also remember to include `System.Numerics` namespace to use `Complex` class for complex numbers.

Here is an example of how you could perform this conversion in PascalABC.NET using the System.Drawing namespace. This is not a fully optimized solution, but it will give you an idea of how the conversion could be done.

In this example, for simplicity, each pixel of each frame of the Tiff image is checked: if its intensity is above a certain threshold, it is set to white, otherwise to black. Adjust this to your needs.

Please note, this code just provides a simple way to convert image colors and does not handle exceptions. You might need to install System.Drawing.Common NuGet package for .NET Core or .NET 5.0.

```csharp
using (Tiff inputTiffImage = Tiff.Open("input.tiff", "r"))
{
    Tiff outputTiff = Tiff.Open("output.tiff", "w");

    int pageNumber = 0;
    do
    {
        FieldValue[] value = inputTiffImage.GetField(TiffTag.IMAGEWIDTH);
        int width = value[0].ToInt();

        value = inputTiffImage.GetField(TiffTag.IMAGELENGTH);
        int height = value[0].ToInt();

        byte[] buffer = new byte[width * height];
        for (int j = 0; j < height; j++)
        {
            inputTiffImage.ReadScanline(buffer, j);
            for (int i = 0; i < width; i++)
            {
                byte pixelValue = buffer[i];
                if (pixelValue > 128) // adjust threshold to your needs
                {
                    buffer[i] = 255; // white
                }
                else
                {
                    buffer[i] = 0; // black
                }
            }
            outputTiff.WriteScanline(buffer, j);
        }

        outputTiff.SetField(TiffTag.COMPRESSION, Compression.CCITTFAX3);
        outputTiff.SetField(TiffTag.IMAGEWIDTH, width);
        outputTiff.SetField(TiffTag.IMAGELENGTH, height);
        outputTiff.SetField(TiffTag.ROWSPERSTRIP, height);
        outputTiff.SetField(TiffTag.XRESOLUTION, inputTiffImage.GetField(TiffTag.XRESOLUTION));
        outputTiff.SetField(TiffTag.YRESOLUTION, inputTiffImage.GetField(TiffTag.YRESOLUTION));
        outputTiff.SetField(TiffTag.PHOTOMETRIC, Photometric.MINISWHITE);
        outputTiff.SetField(TiffTag.RESOLUTIONUNIT, inputTiffImage.GetField(TiffTag.RESOLUTIONUNIT));

        if (!outputTiff.WriteDirectory())
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"Could not write TIFF directory for page {pageNumber}.");
        }
        pageNumber++;
    } while (inputTiffImage.ReadDirectory());

    outputTiff.Close();
}
```
This code uses the BitMiracle.LibTiff library and assumes all images are grayscale. If your images are in color, you will need to first convert them into grayscale with ColorToGrayScale method which you can find examples online.

In this solution, we are using a recursive function `IsPalindrome` and within that we are first cleaning the string, removing the whitespace, punctuation and transforming to lower case in order to ignore case-sensitivity. Then we are using two pointers/indices, `start` and `end`, to compare characters from both ends. If the characters don’t match then we immediately return false. If they do match, we continue with the next set of characters by calling `IsPalindrome` recursively, but this time with `start + 1` and `end - 1` until `start` is greater than or equal to `end`.

Here's a PascalABC.NET program:

```CSharp
using System;
using System.Linq;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Enter a string:");
        string input = Console.ReadLine();
        bool isPalindrome = IsPalindrome(input, 0, input.Length - 1);
        Console.WriteLine("Is the string a palindrome? " + (isPalindrome ? "Yes" : "No"));
    }

    static bool IsPalindrome(string str, int start, int end)
    {
        // base case
        if(start >= end)
        {
            return true;
        }

        // clean the string: remove whitespace, punctuation and make lowercase
        str = new string(str.Where(c => char.IsLetterOrDigit(c)).ToArray()).ToLower();

        // compare characters from both ends
        if(str[start] != str[end]) 
        {
            return false;
        }

        // if characters match, continue with remaining string
        return IsPalindrome(str, start + 1, end - 1);
    }
}
```

When you run this program, it lets you input a string, then it will tell you whether or not it is a palindrome.

Please also note that it would be more efficient to create the cleaned string first then call the recursive function to check if it's a palindrome, instead of cleaning it in each function call.

Sure, here is the implementation of given Python function, `complex_sort`, in PascalABC.NET:

```csharp
public static List<int> ComplexSort(List<int> lst, int n, int m)
{
    List<int> newList = new List<int>(new int[lst.Count]);
    List<int> sortedIndices = Enumerable.Range(0, lst.Count)
                                         .Where(i => i % n == 0 || i % m == 0)
                                         .OrderByDescending(i => i)
                                         .ToList();

    foreach (int i in sortedIndices)
    {
        newList[i] = lst[i] * i;
    }

    return newList;
}
```

Taking a look at the code at a high level, it is creating and manipulating a list of integers in a special way. `newList` is created with a size equal to the input list `lst` and filled with zeroes. `sortedIndices` is a list of numbers from 0 to the count of `lst` in descending order, but only if they are multiples of `n` or `m`. This is achieved using `Enumerable.Range`, `Where`, and `OrderByDescending` methods from LINQ (Language Integrated Query). 

Then, for each of the indices in `sortedIndices`, it multiplies the corresponding element in the input list, `lst`, by the index, and assigns the result to the same index of `newList`.

So, the output of this function, `newList`, is a list filled with the products of the selected elements from the input list and their indices. The selected elements are those at positions multiple of either `n` or `m`. For all other positions, the value in `newList` is 0, as it was initially filled with zeroes.

You can determine the version of the .NET Compact Framework installed on a device through a .NET application by checking the value of the `Environment.Version` property.

Here's a PascalABC.NET example:

```csharp
Version version = Environment.Version;
Console.WriteLine("Version: " + version.ToString());

// Output would be similar to "Version: 2.0.50727.1434"
```

The first two numbers of `version.ToString()` will give you the major and minor version numbers of the .NET compact framework.

But unfortunately, this method does not give you details about service packs.

An alternate way (but more complex) to determine the exact version number (including service packs installed) is to query the registry of the device. Here is a sample code:

```csharp
using (RegistryKey key = Registry.LocalMachine.OpenSubKey(@"\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\.NETCompactFramework"))
{
    if (key != null)
    {
        string[] installedVersions = key.GetValueNames();
        // the installed versions should now contain an array of strings where each 
        // item is a version of the .NET Compact Framework installed on the device
        foreach (string version in installedVersions)
        {
            Console.WriteLine(version);
        }
    }
}
```
The registry key `\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\.NETCompactFramework` should contain values indicating the installed versions of the .NET Compact Framework. The last value appearing after the last backslash character is the version number. Again, service packs are not displayed. Microsoft has not provided a straightforward way of programmatically detecting service pack levels of the .NET CF installed on a device.

