Yes, it is possible to write an SNMP agent using managed code, specifically PascalABC.NET. There are libraries available that can help you in this process. Being able to run your custom agent along with the Windows SNMP service will depend on the design of your particular application.

In terms of libraries, there are several open-source and commercial PascalABC.NET SNMP libraries available. Some popular choices include:

1. **SharpSnmpLib:** This is a free, open-source library. It provides both SNMP agent and manager functionality, and supports SNMP v1, v2c, and v3. You can find it on GitHub, along with an extensive set of examples.

2. **SNMP#NET:** Another free, open-source library. It comes with basic SNMP functionality and is a less complex alternative to SharpSnmpLib. However, it only supports SNMP v1 and v2c.

3. **SNMPc# API:** If you want a commercial library, SNMPc# API is worth considering. It comes with extensive support and documentation. However, it might be overkill if your application doesn't need advanced SNMP features.

As for sample codes, here are a few steps to help you get started using SharpSnmpLib:

```PascalABC.NET
//Instatiate an SNMP agent object
SnmpAgent agent = new SnmpAgent("127.0.0.1", 161);

//Create an SNMP object (representing a piece of data)
SnmpObject obj = new SnmpObject("1.3.6.1.2.1.1.1.0", "Your Data");

//Add the object to the agent
agent.AddObject(obj);

//Start the agent
agent.Start();
```

The code creates an SNMP agent on localhost port 161, creates an SNMP object with a specified OID and data string (your data), and adds the object to the agent. Once the agent starts running, you can use an SNMP manager to query the agent for the object's data.

Remember that building an SNMP agent will involve managing incoming SNMP GET, SET, and TRAP requests, which might not be straightforward. That's why a solid library can be very helpful.

In PascalABC.NET, `ref` and `out` keywords are used with function parameters to pass the actual reference of a variable to a function rather than a copy of the variable's value.

`ref` keyword: When you use `ref` keyword, you need to initialize the variable before passing it to the function. Any changes made to this variable in a function will affect the original variable.

For example:
```csharp
public void Update(ref int x)
{
    x = 10;
}
int num = 5;
Update(ref num); // Now, num becomes 10.
```
In this case, `num` variable is passed to function `Update()` by reference. So, any changes made to `x` in the function `Update()` are also reflected in `num`.

`out` keyword: `out` keyword also allows you to pass the actual reference of a variable to a function, but the variable doesn’t need to be initialized before passing because it must be initialized in the function before it is returned.

For example:
```csharp
public void Create(out int y)
{
    y = 20;
}
int num;
Create(out num); // Now, num becomes 20.
```

In this case, `num` variable is passed to function `Create()` by reference. Since `num` is not initialized when it is passed to `Create()`, it must be initialized in `Create()` before it is returned. Otherwise, you get a compile-time error.

Key differences:
1. `ref` requires that the variable be initialized before it is passed.
2. `out` requires that the variable be initialized in the function it is passed to before it is returned. 

In VB.Net, `ByRef` corresponds to `ref` in PascalABC.NET and `ByVal` corresponds to passing parameter without `ref` and `out` in PascalABC.NET. There is no direct equivalent of `out` keyword in VB.NET but you can achieve similar functionality with `ByRef`.

There doesn't appear to be a built-in "Backup" method in the DirectoryServices namespace of .NET Framework. You would have to manually read and save the data yourself.

However, it looks like you can use the Process class to call the command line utility `ntdsutil` which is able to backup Active Directory.

This example creates `ntdsutil.bat`, then starts the process to run this batch file :

```csharp
using System.Diagnostics;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        using (var writer = new System.IO.StreamWriter(@"C:\path\ntdsutil.bat"))
        {
            writer.WriteLine("\"C:\\Windows\\System32\\ntdsutil.exe\" \"ac in ntds\" \"ifm\" \"create full C:\\path\\Backup\" quit quit");
            writer.Close();
        }

        var process = new Process
        {
            StartInfo = new ProcessStartInfo
            {
                FileName = @"C:\path\ntdsutil.bat",
                RedirectStandardOutput = true,
                UseShellExecute = false,
                CreateNoWindow = true,
            }
        };
        process.Start();
        string output = process.StandardOutput.ReadToEnd();
        process.WaitForExit();
        System.Console.WriteLine(output);
    }
}
```

You have to replace `"C:\\path\\Backup"` and `"C:\\path\\ntdsutil.bat"` with your own paths. Also be sure that user who runs this script has permissions to execute `ntdsutil` and write to specified paths.

As always, make sure paths and permissions are correct and secure when working with system utilities and file IO.

This will initiate a backup of the Active Directory database and log the output of the command.

However `ntdsutil` utility is as good as the built-in Active Directory backup feature of Windows Server, which offers a more complete backup solution such as backing up SYSVOL folder, GPOs, DHCP settings, etc.

