To achieve this in PascalABC.NET, you can use the `Monitor.TryEnter` method which is non-blocking and allows you to specify a timeout. This is what you would want to do if you desire to skip the locked code after a certain amount of time:

```csharp
object obj = new object();

if (Monitor.TryEnter(obj, TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(500)))  // 500 ms timeout
{
    try
    {
        // Locked successfully, place your code here
    }
    finally
    {
        Monitor.Exit(obj);
    }
}
else
{
    // The object was locked, place your alternative code here
}
```
The code above attempts to lock the object `obj`. If `obj` is already locked, `Monitor.TryEnter` will wait up to 500 milliseconds for the lock to be released. If the lock is not released within that time, the method will return `false`, and the code block in the else section will be executed.

Without a timeout, it would look like this:

```csharp
object obj = new object();

if (Monitor.TryEnter(obj))  // No timeout
{
    try
    {
        // Locked successfully, place your code here
    }
    finally
    {
        Monitor.Exit(obj);
    }
}
else
{
    // The object was locked, place your alternative code here
}
```

In this case, `Monitor.TryEnter` will return immediately with `false` if the lock is held by another thread.

Finally, always use a `try/finally` block to ensure that you release the lock, otherwise a deadlock situation might occur if an exception is thrown inside the locked code block.

