To properly clean up Excel Interop objects, you should remember to call Marshal.FinalReleaseComObject on each COM Interop object you’ve finished using. This tells the .NET runtime’s COM Interop layer to decrement the object’s reference count and eventually remove it from memory.

It's also very important to make sure that if an exception is thrown you catch it and clean up anyways. If you don't then you can leave an instance of Excel running in memory, which can be harmful.

Here's example code snippet that might guide you to do it correctly:

```
_excelApp = new Excel.Application();    
try
{    
    _workbook = _excelApp.Workbooks.Open(_fileName, ReadOnly: true);    
    _worksheet = (Excel.Worksheet)_workbook.Sheets[1];

    // your code here 

}
finally
{
    if (_worksheet != null)
        Marshal.FinalReleaseComObject(_worksheet);
    if (_workbook != null)
    {
        _workbook.Close();
        Marshal.FinalReleaseComObject(_workbook);
    }
    if (_excelApp != null)
    {
        _excelApp.Quit();
        Marshal.FinalReleaseComObject(_excelApp);
    }
    _worksheet = null;
    _workbook = null;
    _excelApp = null;
    GC.Collect();
    GC.WaitForPendingFinalizers();
    GC.Collect();
    GC.WaitForPendingFinalizers();
}```

In the above code:
- Excel.Application, Excel.Workbooks, and Excel.Worksheet are instances of COM Interop objects.
- Marshal.FinalReleaseComObject allows the .NET runtime to release an RCW irrespective of how many references to it remain.
- GC.Collect() is used to start garbage collection, which collects and frees up the memory of unreferenced objects.
- GC.WaitForPendingFinalizers is used to suspend the current thread until the thread that is processing the queue of finalizers has emptied that queue. 

This method should ensure that Excel processes are not left hanging in the background. However, you should be aware that you must properly clean up any time you are done with an interop object. This includes objects such as Range that you may not think to clean up. For example:

```csharp
