Below is a potential implementation for a doubly linked list in PascalABC.NET and a method to reverse it in place.

```csharp
public class Node
{
    public int Value;
    public Node Next;
    public Node Previous;

    public Node(int value)
    {
        Value = value;
    }
}

public class LinkedList
{
    private Node _head;
    private Node _tail;

    // Time complexity: O(1)
    // Space complexity: O(1)
    public void InsertAtBeginning(int value)
    {
        var newNode = new Node(value);

        if (_head == null)
        {
            _head = _tail = newNode;
        }
        else
        {
            newNode.Next = _head;
            _head.Previous = newNode;
            _head = newNode;
        }
    }

    // Time complexity: O(n)
    // Space complexity: O(1)
    public void Display()
    {
        var currentNode = _head;
        while (currentNode != null)
        {
            Console.Write(currentNode.Value + " ");
            currentNode = currentNode.Next;
        }
        Console.WriteLine();
    }

    // Time complexity: O(n)
    // Space complexity: O(1)
    public void Reverse()
    {
        var currentNode = _head;
        Node tempNode = null;

        // Flip Previous and Next for all nodes
        while (currentNode != null)
        {
            tempNode = currentNode.Previous;
            currentNode.Previous = currentNode.Next;
            currentNode.Next = tempNode;

            // Move to the next node (now previous)
            currentNode = currentNode.Previous;
        }

        // If the list was not empty
        if (tempNode != null)
        {
            _head = tempNode.Previous;
        }
    }
}
```
The InsertAtBeginning method has a time and space complexity of O(1) since it only involves reassigning a few new pointers, regardless of the size of the list.

The Display method has a time complexity of O(n), where n is the number of nodes in the list, because it needs to traverse the whole list to print it out. Its space complexity is O(1) since it only uses one additional node variable to iterate through the list.

The Reverse method also has a time complexity of O(n) because it needs to traverse the entire list to reverse the next and previous pointers for each node. Its space complexity is still O(1) since we only use a single extra node, even though we are reversing the list.

