Here's an example of how you can write a method in PascalABC.NET that returns a list of strings containing only elements of an input string array that start with the letter 'a' and are followed by a vowel:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

class Program
{
    static List<string> GetStringsStartingWithAAndVowel(string[] inputArray)
    {
        List<string> result = new List<string>();

        foreach (string element in inputArray)
        {
            if (element.StartsWith("a", StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase)) // Check if the element starts with 'a' (case-insensitive)
            {
                char nextCharacter = element[1];

                if (IsVowel(nextCharacter)) // Check if the next character is a vowel
                {
                    result.Add(element);
                }
            }
        }

        return result;
    }

    static bool IsVowel(char c)
    {
        char lowerC = char.ToLower(c);

        return lowerC == 'a' || lowerC == 'e' || lowerC == 'i' || lowerC == 'o' || lowerC == 'u';
    }

    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        string[] inputArray = { "apple", "banana", "ant", "orange", "elephant", "airplane" };
        List<string> result = GetStringsStartingWithAAndVowel(inputArray);

        foreach (string element in result)
        {
            Console.WriteLine(element);
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, the `GetStringsStartingWithAAndVowel` method takes an input array of strings and returns a `List<string>` containing only the elements that start with 'a' and are followed by a vowel.

The method iterates over each element of the input array and checks if it starts with 'a' (case-insensitive) using the `StartsWith` method. If it does, it then checks if the second character (at index 1) is a vowel by calling the `IsVowel` method. If both conditions are met, the element is added to the result list.

The `IsVowel` method is a helper method that checks if a given character is a vowel. It converts the character to lowercase using `ToLower` and then checks if it matches any of the vowels 'a', 'e', 'i', 'o', or 'u'.

In the `Main` method, an example input array is defined and passed to the `GetStringsStartingWithAAndVowel` method. The resulting list is then printed to the console.

