Here's an example of how you can write a function in PascalABC.NET to find the two numbers in a given array that sum up to a target value:

```csharp
public static void FindNumbers(int[] nums, int target)
{
    // Check if the array length is within the required range
    if (nums.Length < 10 || nums.Length > 20)
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Array length must be between 10 and 20");
        return;
    }

    // Create a dictionary to store the complement of each number
    Dictionary<int, int> complements = new Dictionary<int, int>();

    for (int i = 0; i < nums.Length; i++)
    {
        int complement = target - nums[i];

        // Check if the complement already exists in the dictionary
        if (complements.ContainsKey(complement))
        {
            Console.WriteLine("Two numbers found: {0} and {1}", nums[i], complement);
            return;
        }

        // Add the current number and its index as a key-value pair to the dictionary
        complements[nums[i]] = i;
    }

    Console.WriteLine("No two numbers in the array sum up to the target value");
}
```

You can use this function by passing in an array of integers and the target value. It will check if the array length is within the required range and then iterate through the array to find the two numbers that sum up to the target value. If such numbers are found, it will print them. If not, it will indicate that no two numbers in the array sum up to the target value.
